window.POCKET_DOCS=[{"title": "Pocket Editor documentation", "page": "Start here", "url": "index.html", "text": "# Pocket Editor documentation\n\nA complete local guide to the editor, its bundled plugins, and building your own tools. Created by **Somi Douth** · [somidouth@gmail.com](mailto:somidouth@gmail.com).\n\n"}, {"title": "Start in three steps", "page": "Start here", "url": "index.html#start-in-three-steps", "text": "## Start in three steps\n\n1. Launch `python3 -B pocket_editor.py` from the project directory.\n2. Open files or a project, then use Ctrl+P for file search and Ctrl+Shift+P for commands.\n3. Explore Extensions for plugins, and Help for read-only author and contact information.\n\n"}, {"title": "Choose your guide", "page": "Start here", "url": "index.html#choose-your-guide", "text": "## Choose your guide\n\n- [Using the editor](editor.html) — Editor\n- [Plugin catalog](plugins.html) — Plugins\n- [Project Terminal](terminal.html) — Terminal\n- [AI Chat & Code](ai.html) — AI\n- [Your own AI plugin](custom-ai.html) — AI\n- [Plugin development](plugin-development.html) — Developers\n- [AI builder prompt](build-your-ai.html) — Developers\n- [PHP tools & examples](php.html) — Languages\n- [Shortcuts & troubleshooting](reference.html) — Reference\n- [Team Projects & Git](team-work.html) — Collaboration\n- [Full project handbook](handbook.html) — Reference\n\n"}, {"title": "Offline by design", "page": "Start here", "url": "index.html#offline-by-design", "text": "## Offline by design\n\nOpen this HTML file in a browser. No server, account, CDN, JavaScript framework, analytics, or network request is needed to read the documentation. Search works locally when JavaScript is enabled. External reference links require internet access.\n\n"}, {"title": "Verification scope", "page": "Start here", "url": "index.html#verification-scope", "text": "## Verification scope\n\nThe editor and GUI tests were exercised on Linux. Windows/macOS runtime behavior remains unverified. AI integration tests use offline transports; they do not prove provider availability, account access, pricing, or live response quality.\n"}, {"title": "Using Pocket Editor", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html", "text": "# Using Pocket Editor\n\n"}, {"title": "Launch", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#launch", "text": "## Launch\n\n```sh\ncd pocket-editor\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py\n```\n\nOpen a file with `python3 -B pocket_editor.py path/to/file.py`. Start without plugins with `python3 -B pocket_editor.py --safe-mode`.\n\nOn Windows use `py pocket_editor.py`; on macOS use `python3 pocket_editor.py` with a Python installation that includes Tkinter. These platforms have not yet been verified. Debian requires Tkinter (`python3-tk`). Black must be available in the interpreter running the editor; it was available on the development computer during verification.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Appearance and preferences", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#appearance-and-preferences", "text": "## Appearance and preferences\n\nThe interface uses a consistent charcoal palette, a compact New/Open/Save toolbar, a compact Extensions dropdown and optional toolbar shortcuts, wrapping file tabs with close buttons and unsaved markers, and a detailed status bar. Settings → Preferences changes document font, tabs, line numbers, and wrapping. Document font changes do not alter UI or gutter fonts. Code Theme controls the document color scheme independently of the UI palette.\n\nExtensions → Add to toolbar lets you check only the commands you want as shortcuts. The toolbar starts empty, saves your selections, and stays on one row. Shortcuts that do not fit the window are available in More; all plugin commands remain in Extensions.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Documents and settings", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#documents-and-settings", "text": "## Documents and settings\n\nOpen selects one or more files. Each file has its own tab, edits, undo history, cursor, and formatting. Opening the same file again selects its existing tab. New creates another untitled tab. Click × or press Ctrl+W (Cmd+W on macOS) to close the current file; modified files offer Save, Discard, or Cancel. Canceling Save As or a failed save keeps the file open. Save writes the selected file; File → Save All saves edited and untitled files. Quitting checks every open document. Plain-text saving still requires a formatted copy to preserve rich-text styles.\n\nPlain UTF-8 saves retain the detected BOM and newline convention. Mixed newline conventions are normalized on opening. Saves and settings use a temporary sibling file and atomic replacement; existing symlinks are followed.\n\nUse Rich Text → Save Formatted Copy for `.pocket` documents. File → Save writes plain text. Unsaved formatting is protected by a separate discard confirmation. Rich formatting operations do not have formatting undo; text changes have Tk undo/redo. Word `.docx` support is not implemented.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Command palette", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#command-palette", "text": "## Command palette\n\nPress **Ctrl+Shift+P** (**Cmd+Shift+P** on macOS), or choose **Edit → Command Palette…**. Type words from a command name or menu category, use ↑/↓ to select, and press Enter to run. Escape closes without making changes. Plugin commands and toolbar toggles appear automatically from the live menus; reopen the palette after loading new plugins. Disabled commands are excluded, and the palette works in safe mode.\n\nThe bundled **editor_commands** plugin adds 14 everyday commands: Duplicate Line / Selection, Move Lines Up/Down, Delete Lines, Trim Trailing Whitespace, Go to Line, Copy File/Folder Path, Next/Previous Tab, Close Other Tabs, and Zoom In/Out/Reset. Line commands act on selected lines or the cursor's line and support Undo. Close Other Tabs checks unsaved changes and stops on cancellation or save failure. Zoom changes persist in editor settings. Load New Plugins or restart to enable newly installed commands; they are available from Extensions and the palette.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Session restore and draft recovery", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#session-restore-and-draft-recovery", "text": "## Session restore and draft recovery\n\nNormal application launch restores saved file tabs, the active tab, cursor positions, and vertical scroll positions. While the editor is running, a local snapshot is written every five seconds to `pocket_editor_session.json` beside its settings file. After an interrupted session, a prompt offers to restore unsaved text and rich-text styles; original files remain unchanged until you save. Edits made since the last snapshot may be lost. Undo history is retained while switching tabs, but is not restored across application restarts.\n\nNormal closing still asks about unsaved changes. Discarded edits and untitled documents are omitted from the clean session; saved files reopen from disk on the next launch. Missing files are skipped with a notice. Malformed snapshots are backed up as `pocket_editor_session.invalid.json`. A file lock keeps a second editor instance from overwriting the first instance's recovery data; that instance runs without session persistence. The snapshot contains local document contents, including unsaved drafts.\n\nUse `python3 -B pocket_editor.py --no-session` to launch without restoring or writing a session. A file supplied on the command line opens after session restoration. Run `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_editor_session` for the persistence and recovery checks.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Quick Open", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#quick-open", "text": "## Quick Open\n\nPress **Ctrl+P** (**Cmd+P** on macOS), choose **File → Quick Open…**, or search for Quick Open in the command palette. Type part of a filename or relative path; multiple search words and case-insensitive matching are supported. Use ↑/↓ and Enter, or double-click a result. Escape cancels. Files already open select their existing tab, preserving edits.\n\nThe search root comes from Project Explorer's selected project, otherwise the current file's folder, otherwise a folder picker. With no current file, the picker remembers its last selected folder for that editor session. Reopening Quick Open refreshes the index. File discovery runs in a background thread and skips `.git`, `.hg`, `.svn`, `node_modules`, `__pycache__`, `.venv`, `venv`, and `vendor`; directory symlinks are not traversed. Up to 50,000 files and 20,000 directories are scanned, and the first 200 matching results are shown. If a limit is reached, narrow the project folder. Unreadable folders are reported in the picker footer.\n\nRun `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_quick_open test_command_palette` for discovery, cancellation, existing-tab reuse, and palette checks.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Split editor", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#split-editor", "text": "## Split editor\n\nChoose **View → Split Editor Right**, or find that command with Ctrl+Shift+P. The current file stays on the left and another open file appears on the right. With only one document, the editor creates an empty second tab. Drag the divider to resize the panes. Both panes are editable and scroll independently, with line numbers and filename/unsaved indicators.\n\nClick a pane to activate its document. Save, Undo, palette commands, plugins, and syntax diagnostics use that active document. Choosing a tab already visible focuses its pane; another tab replaces the active pane's file. **View → Focus Other Pane** switches panes. **View → Close Split** returns to one pane without closing any tabs or discarding edits. Closing a file still checks unsaved changes and collapses the split when only one document remains. Font, tab width, wrapping, and line-number preferences apply to both panes. Session restore retains documents and cursor positions; the split arrangement itself is not restored.\n\nVerify with `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_split_editor`.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "About and tab appearance", "page": "Using the editor", "url": "editor.html#about-and-tab-appearance", "text": "## About and tab appearance\n\n**Help → About Pocket Editor…** (also in the command palette) shows **Somi Douth**, **somidouth@gmail.com**, a short editor description, and a Python/Tkinter technology credit. Publisher information is read-only; the About window provides Copy details and Close. Old local About override files do not replace the published author information.\n\nDocument tabs now use consistent active/inactive backgrounds and readable titles: white for selected clean files, muted light text for inactive files, and amber for unsaved files. Close buttons match the tab background. Selected, hovered, disabled, and inactive notebook tabs have explicit contrasting colors across plugin windows, including Terminal Settings and Plugin Builder. Split-pane headings use matching active/inactive title colors.\n\n"}, {"title": "Bundled plugin catalog", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html", "text": "# Bundled plugin catalog\n\nEvery loadable plugin in the source tree is listed below. Helpers beginning with `_` are not standalone plugins. Commands and service/event names are extracted from the Python syntax tree without executing plugins. Event lists show statically declared names, not every dynamic event.\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugins", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#plugins", "text": "## Plugins\n\nPython files in `pocket_editor_plugins/` define `register(api)`. Plugin IDs are filename stems. Plugins load in filename order and repeated Load New Plugins does not register them twice.\n\nThe API supports commands, text access/replacement, paths, events, services, and discovery. `api.list_services()` returns qualified service names. `api.on_cleanup(callback)` registers cleanup for failed registration or editor exit. Event callbacks and service calls execute on the GUI thread. Event payload key `source` is reserved. Plugins are trusted Python code; plugins that access private editor internals must register cleanup for their own side effects.\n\nThe terminal is an output-only shell runner. Its output queue and retained text are bounded; retained text is limited to 200,000 characters and individual long lines retain their last 8,192 characters. Stop and application exit terminate the process group on POSIX. Deliberately detached processes are outside this guarantee. Windows process-tree handling needs platform verification.\n\nRegex searches run in a disposable Python process with a 0.5-second execution timeout and a 100,000-match limit. A search may briefly pause the GUI while that bounded operation completes. Syntax highlighting pauses above 300,000 document characters.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugin Builder", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#plugin-builder", "text": "## Plugin Builder\n\nUse **Extensions → Plugin Builder…** to preview, create, and load a command, text transform, tool window, or event/service plugin. Existing plugin files are protected from overwriting. See [Plugin development and API reference](PLUGIN_DEVELOPMENT.md) for the tutorial, templates, events, services, cleanup, testing, and reload workflow.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugin Manager", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#plugin-manager", "text": "## Plugin Manager\n\nChoose **Extensions → Plugin Manager…**, **Settings → Plugin Manager…**, or find it through Ctrl+Shift+P. Search by plugin ID or status, select a plugin, and inspect its path, registered commands, subscribed events, services, and latest loading or command error. Open source opens the file in an editor tab. Refresh rescans installed plugins; Load new plugins retries failed registrations and loads newly enabled plugins that have not already loaded.\n\nEnable/Disable persists startup preferences in `disabled_plugins` in the editor settings. Disabling a loaded plugin takes effect after restarting; it does not remove running callbacks or existing menu commands mid-session. Enabling an unloaded plugin allows Load new plugins to load it. Edited plugins that already loaded require a restart. Failed settings writes roll back the requested toggle. Runtime errors remain visible until restart; successful registration clears a previous loading error.\n\nIn `--safe-mode`, use **Settings → Plugin Manager…** to disable a broken plugin, then restart normally. Loading remains disabled while in safe mode. The manager reports the current process's registered events and services, not a dependency manifest or every event a plugin might emit. Helpers beginning with `_` are excluded.\n\nRun `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_plugin_manager` for startup preferences, safe mode, plugin communication inspection, error retry, and settings-failure checks.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Inline syntax diagnostics", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#inline-syntax-diagnostics", "text": "## Inline syntax diagnostics\n\nThe **syntax_diagnostics** plugin checks saved Python (`.py`, `.pyw`, `.pyi`) and JSON (`.json`) filenames after a 450 ms typing pause. Checks use the current unsaved buffer, run in a background worker, and never execute user code. The first syntax error gets a red underline and a clickable line/column message below the editor. Fixing the error clears the marker. Results from a changed or switched document are discarded.\n\nSearch the palette for **Check Syntax**, **Go to Syntax Error**, or **Toggle Automatic Syntax Checks**. Manual checks remain available when automatic checks are off; that toggle lasts for the current session. Untitled and unsupported filenames are not checked automatically. Checks skip files above 200,000 characters. Python uses the running interpreter's compiler; JSON uses Python's built-in JSON decoder. These are syntax checks, not type checking, undefined-variable analysis, or project-wide diagnostics. Undo, saving, and file contents are unaffected by checking.\n\nOther plugins can request an asynchronous check through `api.call_service(\"syntax_diagnostics.check\")`, inspect `api.call_service(\"syntax_diagnostics.get_status\")`, and subscribe with `api.on(\"syntax_diagnostics.updated\", callback)`. The completion event contains `source`, `path` (string or None), and `result` with a status/message and line/column for syntax errors. The check service queues work and returns None. Disable the plugin through Plugin Manager if needed.\n\nChoose Extensions → Load New Plugins or restart to enable it. Verify with `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_syntax_diagnostics`.\n\nThe Extensions toolbar dropdown groups commands by plugin to keep the menu compact. Add to toolbar uses the same plugin groups and shared checkmarks as the menubar. Plugins with more than 18 commands continue under More… rather than producing an oversized menu. Search Commands opens the command palette for direct access to every command. The menubar's Plugins menu remains available.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "ai_assistant", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#ai_assistant", "text": "## ai_assistant\n\nBundled free-model AI Chat and AI Code, explicit attachments, Ask/Team workflows, history, reply caps, and provider cooldowns. Uses the packaged tools/ai scripts, with a legacy sibling fallback.\n\n**How to use:** Configure provider credentials through /keys in tools/ai/chat_ai.py or tools/ai/code_ai.py, open AI Chat or AI Code, and Refresh models. Select Ask or Team, explicitly attach files/current document, then Send. Stop cancels the local process. Use Reply tokens only when you intend to raise the requested cap; provider limits still apply.\n\nCommands: `Copy conversation`, `Reset context`, `Refresh models`, `Help & limits`, `AI Chat…`, `AI Code…`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/ai_assistant.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "code_theme", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#code_theme", "text": "## code_theme\n\nDark, Light, Dracula, and Solarized Dark document themes with bounded syntax highlighting. Theme choices do not replace editor chrome.\n\n**How to use:** Open Code Theme, choose a color scheme and language, and close the dialog. Auto selects highlighting from the filename extension. Highlighting stops above 300,000 characters; text remains editable.\n\nCommands: `Code Theme`.\n\nServices: `code_theme.refresh`.\n\nSubscribed events: `document_switched`, `document_changed`, `document_formatted`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/code_theme.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "editor_commands", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#editor_commands", "text": "## editor_commands\n\nFourteen everyday line-editing, tab-navigation, clipboard-path, and zoom commands. Line changes support Undo; tab closing checks unsaved edits.\n\n**How to use:** Place the cursor on a line or select several lines, then invoke a line command from the palette. Selection ending at the next line’s column zero excludes that next line. Go to Line asks for a valid line number. Copy Path needs a saved filename. Zoom preferences persist.\n\nCommands: `Duplicate Line / Selection`, `Move Lines Up`, `Move Lines Down`, `Delete Lines`, `Trim Trailing Whitespace`, `Go to Line…`, `Copy File Path`, `Copy Folder Path`, `Next Tab`, `Previous Tab`, `Close Other Tabs`, `Zoom In`, `Zoom Out`, `Reset Zoom`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/editor_commands.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "git_project", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#git_project", "text": "## git_project\n\nIndependent Git tools for any project: automatic init, shared repository service, local Git settings, clone, origin, status, commits, pull, and push. Optional Explorer integration.\n\n**How to use:** Open Git Project or use Explorer Actions. Browse selects a folder and initializes Git if absent; Set Git Folder prepares a typed path. The repository root is available through ensure_repository/get_repository services. Clone downloads a repository. Set Origin configures its remote. Save files, Refresh, select changes, and Commit Selected. Push publishes; Pull only fast-forwards and requires closed project tabs. Settings edits repository-local author identity, origin, line endings, executable-bit tracking, and commit signing; blank/Inherit uses inherited defaults.\n\nCommands: `Git Project`.\n\nServices: `git_project.open_project`, `git_project.ensure_repository`, `git_project.get_repository`, `git_project.get_project`.\n\nPublished events: `git_project.repository_changed`, `project_changed`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/git_project.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "my_ai_example", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#my_ai_example", "text": "## my_ai_example\n\nIndependent customizable AI tool with Responses/Chat Completions, provider settings, model discovery, explicit file attachments, and local request limits.\n\n**How to use:** Open My AI Example and click Settings. Select a preset/API format, enter an API key if required, refresh models or type an exact model ID, and set limits. Use + Files for up to four UTF-8 snapshots and the active-tab checkbox for unsaved editor context. Enter sends, Shift+Enter adds a line, and Open reply creates a new unsaved document.\n\nCommands: `My AI Example…`.\n\nServices: `my_ai_example.get_status`.\n\nPublished events: `my_ai_example.reply`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/my_ai_example.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "php_completion", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#php_completion", "text": "## php_completion\n\nPHP completion through the local Intelephense language server, with snippets, project/library configuration, and stale-result protection.\n\n**How to use:** Choose Open Project, open a PHP-family file, and press Ctrl+Space or type an identifier. Navigate suggestions with arrows, accept with Enter/Tab, and dismiss with Escape. Settings controls automatic delay, limits, PHP version, and additional library folders. Use Reindex PHP Project after external filesystem changes.\n\nCommands: `PHP Completion`, `PHP Completion Settings`, `PHP Completion Help`, `Reindex PHP Project`.\n\nServices: `php_completion.complete`, `php_completion.status`.\n\nSubscribed events: `document_changed`, `document_saved`, `project_changed`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/php_completion.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "php_formatter", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#php_formatter", "text": "## php_formatter\n\nPrettier PHP formatting of the active buffer with one-step Undo; does not save the document to disk.\n\n**How to use:** Open a PHP-family document, invoke Format PHP, review the buffer, and Save separately. Use PHP Format Settings for print width and target version. Missing local Node dependencies or invalid syntax leave the text unchanged. Undo reverts a formatting change.\n\nCommands: `Format PHP`, `PHP Format Settings`.\n\nServices: `php_formatter.format`, `php_formatter.busy`.\n\nPublished events: `document_formatted`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/php_formatter.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "plugin_builder", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#plugin_builder", "text": "## plugin_builder\n\nPreview and create a simple command, text transform, tool window, or event/service plugin. Exclusive creation protects existing files.\n\n**How to use:** Choose a unique lowercase plugin ID, command label, description, and template. Inspect Code preview and Documentation, then Create & load. A source tab opens for editing. Restart after changing an already-loaded plugin. The Plugin communication page lists loaded IDs/services; Refresh services updates it.\n\nCommands: `Plugin Builder…`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/plugin_builder.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "project_explorer", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#project_explorer", "text": "## project_explorer\n\nProject tree with Open/Save/Load project controls, file operations, and launchers for independent Git and hosted teamwork.\n\n**How to use:** Choose Open Project and select a folder. Expand the tree and double-click a file, or press Enter. Use Actions or the context menu for New File, New Folder, Rename, Delete, and Refresh. Rename is also available through F2. File operations require explicit UI actions and retain unsaved-file protection.\n\nCommands: `Open Project Folder…`, `Load Project…`, `Save Project`, `Save Project As…`, `Git Project…`, `Team Project…`, `Open File`, `New File…`, `New Folder…`, `Rename…`, `Delete…`, `Refresh`, `Project Explorer`, `Open Project`, `Load Project`, `Save Project As`.\n\nServices: `project_explorer.get_project`, `project_explorer.open_project`.\n\nSubscribed events: `project_changed`.\n\nPublished events: `project_changed`, `document_changed`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/project_explorer.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "project_terminal", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#project_terminal", "text": "## project_terminal\n\nOutput-only system-shell runner with stop controls, per-project commands, ANSI rendering, semantic categories, and portable appearance preferences.\n\n**How to use:** Set a working directory and command, then Run. Stop terminates the process group where supported. Save Project Command remembers the command for that directory. Settings configures presets, ANSI colors, categories, font/encoding, and force-color. This runner has no interactive input.\n\nCommands: `Project Terminal`, `Project Terminal Settings…`.\n\nServices: `project_terminal.show`, `project_terminal.get_settings`.\n\nPublished events: `terminal_settings_changed`, `command_finished`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/project_terminal.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "python_formatter", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#python_formatter", "text": "## python_formatter\n\nPython formatting using optional Black, with settings and Undo. Black must be installed in the editor’s interpreter.\n\n**How to use:** Open a Python document and choose Format Python. Review the buffer, use Undo if needed, and Save separately. Python Format Settings selects a line length from 40 to 200. Black can also use project configuration and fmt: off regions. A missing Black dependency produces an error rather than silently changing tools.\n\nCommands: `Format Python`, `Python Format Settings`.\n\nServices: `python_formatter.format`.\n\nPublished events: `document_formatted`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/python_formatter.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "rich_text", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#rich_text", "text": "## rich_text\n\nText styles and formatted .pocket copies. Plain-text Save does not preserve formatting. Text Undo is available; formatting Undo is not implemented.\n\n**How to use:** Select text before applying Bold, Italic, Underline, Strikeout, font, size, text color, or highlighting. Paragraph alignment affects the relevant paragraphs. Clear Formatting removes rich tags from the selection. Save Formatted Copy creates a .pocket file; Open Formatted loads one into a new tab. Formatting changes require a formatted copy to preserve them.\n\nCommands: `Rich Text`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/rich_text.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "search_replace", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#search_replace", "text": "## search_replace\n\nFind and replace with previous/next navigation, regex options, and bounded regex execution in a disposable process.\n\n**How to use:** Open Search & Replace, enter the search and optional replacement, choose case/regex options, then use Next or Previous. Replace selects the next match before replacing if no matching active result is selected; Replace All works on the active document. Regex replacements support capture references. Invalid expressions and bounded-search failures are shown without replacing the document.\n\nCommands: `Search & Replace`.\n\nPublished events: `document_changed`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/search_replace.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "syntax_diagnostics", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#syntax_diagnostics", "text": "## syntax_diagnostics\n\nNon-executing Python/JSON syntax checks in a worker, error markers, jump-to-error, and a status/check service.\n\n**How to use:** Save or open a Python/JSON filename, then edit. After the debounce pause, the first syntax error is underlined and its message appears below the editor. Click the message or invoke Go to Syntax Error. Check Syntax runs manually; Toggle Automatic Syntax Checks affects this session. Checks skip buffers over 200,000 characters and never execute source.\n\nCommands: `Check Syntax`, `Go to Syntax Error`, `Toggle Automatic Syntax Checks`.\n\nServices: `syntax_diagnostics.get_status`, `syntax_diagnostics.check`.\n\nSubscribed events: `document_changed`, `document_switched`, `document_saved`.\n\nPublished events: `syntax_diagnostics.updated`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/syntax_diagnostics.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "team_project", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#team_project", "text": "## team_project\n\nHost/client project copies, individual keys, portable client invitations, modern group chat, and audio/video/file exchange.\n\n**How to use:** Choose Host a project or Join as client. Host mode shows its actual listen port and suggests a LAN address. Easy Internet Setup detects OS/CPU, installs a checksum-verified user-local cloudflared, and starts a temporary HTTPS relay. Only a verified relay URL is filled into invitations. It also exports setup commands/scripts. Client mode loads an invitation; no client listening port is needed. Details shows local and peer endpoints; the host client list shows last observed IPs/source ports. Under Project Copies, generate a named client key and export a .team-invite using a reachable server URL. Detect My Public IP is at the top for both roles before connecting and uses HTTPS fallback services with visible DNS/network errors; Use HTTPS IP sets the invitation address only after an explicit click. HTTPS and actual inbound reachability must be configured separately. The client loads that invitation, chooses a local copy folder, and clicks Join Team. Use Group Chat and Shared Files to communicate, and Project Copies to pull or send saved file changes.\n\nCommands: `Team Project`.\n\nServices: `team_project.open_project`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/team_project.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "text_formatting", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#text_formatting", "text": "## text_formatting\n\nPreview transformations for selection or document: case changes, whitespace cleanup, indentation, sorting, and duplicate-line removal.\n\n**How to use:** Select a region or leave no selection to target the whole document. Open Text Formatting, choose an operation and indentation width, and review the preview. Apply Formatting performs an undoable change. If the document or selected range changed while the dialog was open, reopen the formatter instead of applying stale results.\n\nCommands: `Text Formatting`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/text_formatting.py)\n\n\n"}, {"title": "word_count", "page": "Plugin catalog", "url": "plugins.html#word_count", "text": "## word_count\n\nCounts words in the selected document and displays the result.\n\n**How to use:** Choose Count words to count whitespace-separated words in the whole active document, including unsaved edits. A text selection does not restrict this count.\n\nCommands: `Count words`.\n\n[View source](pocket_editor_plugins/word_count.py)\n"}, {"title": "Project Terminal", "page": "Project Terminal", "url": "terminal.html", "text": "# Project Terminal\n\nRun commands from a chosen directory using the operating system’s shell. This is an output-only runner: interactive shells, password prompts, full-screen terminal programs, and input forwarding are unsupported. Use Run, Stop, Save Project Command, and Settings. The default directory comes from the project, then the active file, then the current working directory.\n\n"}, {"title": "Project Terminal colors and portable defaults", "page": "Project Terminal", "url": "terminal.html#project-terminal-colors-and-portable-defaults", "text": "## Project Terminal colors and portable defaults\n\nChoose **Project Terminal → Settings…** inside the terminal, or search **Project Terminal Settings…** in the command palette. Editor Dark, Light, and High Contrast presets provide a starting point. Customize background, normal text, command headers, status messages, error exit messages, and all 16 ANSI base/bright colors. Font family/size, output encoding, ANSI rendering, and optional force-color behavior are user settings. Save & apply persists them under `_appearance` in `project_terminal_settings.json`, preserving saved per-project commands.\n\nPortable defaults use UTF-8 decoding, the operating system's monospace font, ANSI rendering enabled, and force-color disabled. The existing system-shell selection stays native to the OS; no Bash-only commands are introduced. If a chosen font is unavailable on another computer, the system monospace font is used. For Windows tools that emit a legacy encoding, choose locale, cp1252, cp850, or cp437 as appropriate. Python child processes receive the selected output encoding. Encoding and force-color changes apply to the next process; appearance changes recolor retained output immediately.\n\nThe renderer handles standard/bright ANSI colors, indexed 256-color and RGB colors, bold, underline, reverse, resets, and sequences split across read chunks. Other terminal controls, including title/clipboard/hyperlink escape commands, are discarded rather than executed; carriage-return progress updates become separate lines. This remains an output-only runner, not a full interactive terminal emulator. Standard error remains merged with standard output; category coloring uses recognizable text patterns rather than claiming to identify the originating stream. Nonzero exit markers use the configured error color.\n\nPrograms may suppress colors when output is piped. Enable **Request colored output from programs (force color)** to set FORCE_COLOR, CLICOLOR_FORCE, and TERM for the child; it also overrides NO_COLOR for that process. Some tools still require their own `--color=always` option. Disabling ANSI interpretation removes ANSI styling from retained text as well as future output. Output remains bounded to 200,000 characters and 8,192 characters per long line, with at most 512 distinct ANSI styles. No OS-specific ANSI rendering library is required.\n\nPlugin communication exposes `project_terminal.get_settings` and emits `terminal_settings_changed` after saving. ANSI interpretation follows the [xterm control-sequence reference](https://invisible-island.net/xterm/ctlseqs/ctlseqs.html). Parser tests are platform-independent; GUI/process tests were run on Linux. Windows/macOS runtime testing remains outstanding.\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_terminal_colors test_editor.EditorTests.test_terminal_output_fallback_stop_and_shutdown\n```\n\nThe main **AI Chat / AI Code** window now keeps the current reply intact instead of deleting its first 300 lines once the transcript exceeds 1,800 lines. Older transcript content is trimmed toward 200,000 characters without cutting the latest assistant/team reply; one large current reply can exceed that target. Code-fence formatting also survives consecutive assistant output chunks. **Reply tokens → Auto** keeps the existing conservative defaults; explicitly choose up to 8,192 for a larger requested reply cap. Provider limits/timeouts can still stop generation, and larger caps can increase usage. No extra provider request or larger default budget is introduced.\n\nTerminal presets now include **GNOME Tango** (inspired by the [GNOME Tango color palette](https://wiki.gnome.org/Design/OS/Colors)) and **Modern Graphite**, a softer dark scheme with restrained blue, green, amber, and magenta ANSI colors. Modern Graphite is the default for new settings; saved custom colors are preserved. The settings dialog previews command/error text and all 16 palette colors before Save & apply. Each preset applies its complete ANSI palette, not just its background. Restore defaults selects Modern Graphite.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Terminal output categories", "page": "Project Terminal", "url": "terminal.html#terminal-output-categories", "text": "## Terminal output categories\n\n**Terminal Settings → Categories** lets you choose separate colors for user commands/messages, system/info messages, warnings, errors, and successful completion. Automatic category coloring is enabled by default and can be disabled independently of ANSI rendering. Plain, unrecognized program output uses the normal foreground color. Settings apply to retained output as well as new lines.\n\nExamples of recognized prefixes are `USER:`, `INPUT:`, `SYSTEM:`, `INFO:`, `DEBUG:`, `WARNING:`, `ERROR:`, and `SUCCESS:` (also bracketed equivalents). Common compiler `file:line: error:` / `warning:` messages and build/test success lines are recognized. Matching is heuristic, not an assertion about process privileges or stdout/stderr origin. Partial lines are reevaluated as chunks arrive. The runner's own directory/status messages, command echoes, and exit markers have explicit categories.\n\nProgram-supplied ANSI colors take precedence over inferred category colors, matching the palette-driven model described in [GNOME Terminal's color documentation](https://help.gnome.org/gnome-terminal/app-colors.html). GNOME palettes alone do not provide this plugin's automatic log-category classification. You can use both features, disable automatic classification to see only application colors, or disable ANSI rendering to use only category colors.\n\n"}, {"title": "AI Chat and Code", "page": "AI Chat & Code", "url": "ai.html", "text": "# AI Chat and Code\n\nThis is the bundled free-model frontend, separate from My AI Example. It uses the packaged `tools/ai/chat_ai.py` and `tools/ai/code_ai.py` backends. Provider credentials are supplied separately by the user.\n\n"}, {"title": "AI Chat and Code", "page": "AI Chat & Code", "url": "ai.html#ai-chat-and-code", "text": "## AI Chat and Code\n\nThe AI window matches the editor’s charcoal theme, blue accents, compact controls, and UI font family, with Chat/Code navigation and a bottom\ncomposer, styled user/assistant cards, lightweight Markdown and code blocks,\nremovable attachment chips, and a welcome screen with draft starters. Enter sends;\nShift+Enter inserts a newline. The composer clears only after a request starts;\na failed start preserves the draft. AI fonts/styles are isolated from editor\nsettings. The sidebar collapses in narrow windows; More keeps Copy, Reset,\nRefresh models and Help accessible. Reset context clears model history while\nkeeping displayed messages available to copy. Copy preserves original Markdown\nand code text even when formatting markers are hidden in the chat display.\n\n[Conversation preview](docs/assets/previews/ai-chat-preview.png) ·\n[Welcome preview](docs/assets/previews/ai-chat-welcome.png) ·\n[Narrow-window preview](docs/assets/previews/ai-chat-narrow.png).\nThese previews use offline example messages; no provider request was sent.\n\n\nOpen **Extensions → AI Chat…** or **AI Code…**. The window has Chat/Code and\nAsk/Team selectors. Ask requires one model and a question. Team shows a lead\nselector and worker list; Ctrl-click workers from different developers, then\nenter the task. Workers run in displayed order. Refresh models reloads available\nmodels. Requests run in a separate process; Cancel stops that process.\n\nAttach current document sends a snapshot including unsaved edits. Attach files\nadds UTF-8 files. Attachments remain until cleared; no document is sent implicitly.\nReplies appear in the output pane and never overwrite a document. Copy conversation\ncopies the retained visible prompts, replies and team output, excluding hidden\nattachment text. Solo histories are separate per mode/model; the latest six turns\nare stored during the session, and Reset conversations clears them. Requests send\nonly the recent complete turns that fit the active fast profile. Omitted history\nis noted in the transcript; earlier visible conversation remains copyable. Team\nruns are independent.\n\nThe plugin uses these conservative **fast presets**, rather than claiming a\nuniversal optimum for every model:\n\n| Limit | Chat | Code |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Task + attachments, UTF-8 | 4 KiB | 12 KiB Ask / 6 KiB Team |\n| Requested reply cap | 512 tokens | 1,536 tokens |\n| Outgoing history | Up to 2 turns / 4 KiB | Up to 3 turns / 8 KiB |\n| Attachments | 2 | 4 |\n| Team workers, excluding lead | 2 | 2 |\n| Ask timeout | 60 seconds | 120 seconds |\n| Team timeout | 120 seconds | 180 seconds |\n\nThe active limits and input usage are shown in the AI window. Input byte counts\ninclude prompt, file headers and separators. Oversized tasks/files are rejected\nwith an explanation, preserving the prompt and previous attachments. Large files\nare read only up to a bounded size. A team request whose source plus Python syntax\ncheck metadata exceeds the backend source budget is rejected before model calls.\nExisting provider-size retry behavior may shorten team context with a visible\nbackend notice. Reply caps can leave large answers incomplete; Code asks for\nfocused patches rather than full-file rewrites. Timeouts stop the local request\nprocess and keep displayed partial output. Model discovery has a 45-second limit.\nThe shared command-line scripts are not changed; the GUI bridge supplies reply\ncaps at runtime to solo, planning, worker and final-answer calls.\n\nAsk makes fewer model calls than Team. These presets target smaller requests and\nshorter replies; actual latency depends on the model/provider. No live-provider\nspeed benchmark was performed. General rationale: [OpenAI latency optimization](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization).\n\nThe plugin uses `tools/ai/chat_ai.py` and `tools/ai/code_ai.py` and their shared\ncredentials. Configure keys and Free plan confirmations via `/keys` in either\nscript before use. Launch the editor again, or select **Load New Plugins**.\nNo API request is made until opening the assistant (model discovery) or Send.\nRun offline coverage with `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_ai_assistant` on a desktop.\n\n### AI capabilities and provider failures\n\nThe AI backend now receives explicit instructions that Pocket Editor supplies only\nprompt/history/attached text. It has no tools to browse a project, read files on\nits own, write files, inspect the machine, run commands/tests, or commit changes.\nIt may explain attachments and propose patches for the user to apply. Any model\nclaim of direct local access is incorrect; model-generated claims do not grant\ncapabilities. Earlier misleading replies are not proof of tool access.\n\nThe normal fast reply caps remain 512 Chat / 1,536 Code tokens. If a model returns\nan empty visible reply, the plugin retries that same call **once**, with up to\n2,048 Chat / 3,072 Code output/reasoning tokens. A notice appears before the retry.\nThis can help reasoning models that exhaust a small output budget, but does not\nguarantee success and may use an extra request/quota. No hidden reasoning is\nshown as the final answer. A second empty result produces a readable error.\n\nHTTP 429 is not automatically retried. The UI displays a provider rate-limit\nmessage, preserves the draft when sending is blocked, and pauses new requests\nusing that provider for its Retry-After interval when available (at most one day)\nor 30 seconds otherwise. The pause is local and cannot reset a provider's quota;\nactual free-tier/shared-provider limits can last longer. Select a different\nprovider or wait. Provider messages do not expose raw Python tracebacks. Failed\nteam planning/finalization is marked as failed even when the command-line backend\ncatches the exception; worker-only failures allow a partial result with an\nexplicit status. A successful size-limit retry does not leave a stale failure.\n\nReplies and team logs use structured text events, so answer lines that resemble\nprotocol markers cannot be mistaken for model lists or history records. Normal\nfast limits, Copy, the chat theme and Stop remain in place. Shared backend scripts\nwere not modified.\n\nSources: [OpenRouter reasoning/output budgets](https://github.com/OpenRouterTeam/docs/blob/main/guides/best-practices/reasoning-tokens.mdx),\n[OpenRouter error handling](https://github.com/OpenRouterTeam/docs/blob/main/api_reference/errors-and-debugging.mdx).\nVerification used offline transports, not live provider requests; provider quota\nrecovery and the reported models' current availability remain unverified.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "Full reply display", "page": "AI Chat & Code", "url": "ai.html#full-reply-display", "text": "## Full reply display\n\nThe current assistant/team reply is preserved while older transcript content is trimmed toward 200,000 characters. Reply tokens → Auto retains the conservative defaults. Choose an explicit cap up to 8,192 when a longer reply is needed; this can increase usage. A larger cap does not override provider quotas or timeouts. No automatic continuation request is made.\n"}, {"title": "Build your own AI Chat/Code plugin", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html", "text": "# Build your own AI Chat/Code plugin\n\n`pocket_editor_plugins/my_ai_example.py` is a working, standalone example using only Python's standard library and the editor's PluginAPI. It does not depend on the bundled free-model AI scripts and does not send requests at startup.\n\n"}, {"title": "Run the example", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#run-the-example", "text": "## Run the example\n\n1. Restart Pocket Editor, or choose Extensions → Load New Plugins.\n2. Open Extensions → My AI Example…, or find it in Ctrl+Shift+P.\n3. Click **Settings**. Choose an OpenAI, Ollama, or OpenRouter provider preset, or choose Custom and select the API format. Enter the provider's exact **model ID**, or click Refresh models and choose a compatible text model. Enter an **API key** where required.\n4. Set the reply-token, input-byte, network-timeout, and session-request limits.\n5. Choose Chat or Code, type a message, and press Send.\n\nFor local Ollama, the default endpoint is `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1/chat/completions`. Run Ollama separately and enter a model ID you have already installed. Leave the key empty for a local server that does not require authentication.\n\nFor OpenRouter, use `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions`, your OpenRouter API key, and a model ID available to your account. Other providers must support this example's OpenAI-compatible `messages`, `max_tokens`, `stream: false`, and text `choices[0].message.content` format. Native Anthropic, Gemini, streaming, tool calls, images, and provider-specific authentication are not implemented by this example; use a compatible gateway or adapt `send_request` for that provider. Some models require different token-limit parameters.\n\nProvider references:\n- [Ollama OpenAI compatibility](https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/docs/api/openai-compatibility.mdx)\n- [OpenRouter chat-completions API](https://openrouter.ai/docs/api/api-reference/chat/send-chat-completion-request)\n- [OpenRouter authentication](https://github.com/OpenRouterTeam/docs/blob/main/api_reference/authentication.mdx)\n\n"}, {"title": "Conversation interface", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#conversation-interface", "text": "## Conversation interface\n\nThe main window keeps the conversation in focus: a compact Chat/Code header, model summary, welcome prompts, distinct user/assistant messages, monospaced fenced code blocks, and a bottom composer with an attachment toggle and Send button. **Settings** opens provider, credential, model discovery, and limit controls in a separate non-modal window. **Done** hides Settings; Save settings persists only non-secret configuration.\n\nPress Enter to send or Shift+Enter for a new line. Welcome prompts only fill the composer; they do not make requests. **Copy conversation** preserves the visible transcript as plain text, including code fences, while **Open reply** copies only the latest reply into a new unsaved editor tab. Reset context clears model history and keeps the visible transcript. Closing the chat hides both its window and Settings, preserving the session for reopening. The layout adapts down to 640 × 540 without changing the editor's shared fonts or styles.\n\n"}, {"title": "Test with an OpenAI Platform API key", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#test-with-an-openai-platform-api-key", "text": "## Test with an OpenAI Platform API key\n\n1. Restart the editor after updating the plugin, then open **My AI Example…**.\n2. Open **Settings**, then choose **Provider → OpenAI**. This selects **OpenAI Responses** and `https://api.openai.com/v1/responses`.\n3. Paste your **OpenAI Platform API key** into the memory-only key field, or launch the editor with `OPENAI_API_KEY` set. A Codex/ChatGPT subscription login token is not a replacement for a Platform API key. This plugin does not read Codex credentials, use subscription allowances, or implement Codex CLI/SDK agents.\n4. Click **Refresh models**. This explicitly calls `GET /v1/models`, does not generate a reply, and counts against the plugin's local request cap. Choose a text/coding model your account can use with Responses, or type its exact API ID. Catalog entries can include non-text models or models requiring different APIs; discovery does not prove request compatibility. No model ID is hardcoded or automatically selected.\n5. Leave **Reasoning → default** initially. Choose low/medium/high only when the model supports that value. Some reasoning models may use the output cap for reasoning and return little or no visible text; increase the cap explicitly if needed. The plugin never retries automatically.\n6. Set a small session request limit, choose Chat or Code, and send a short test prompt such as “Reply with one short sentence.” Keep Attach current tab off for the first test.\n7. Save settings if desired. Keys and conversations are excluded. Check your Platform usage/billing dashboard for actual usage; the local request/token controls are not a monetary budget.\n\nThe Responses request uses `instructions`, text-message `input`, `max_output_tokens`, `stream: false`, and `store: false`; explicit reasoning adds `reasoning.effort`. Conversation context is resent from local memory without previous-response IDs. The reader collects message output-text parts, handles refusals as visible text, reports incomplete replies, and ignores non-text reasoning/tool items. `store: false` requests that the response not be stored for later API retrieval; it is not a promise about all provider retention policies.\n\nAuthentication/access errors, invalid parameters, missing endpoints/models, credits/quota issues, and rate-limit pauses are displayed without revealing error bodies or keys. Refreshing models does not require a model ID; sending does. No request is made merely by choosing a preset or saving settings.\n\nOfficial references: [OpenAI quickstart](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/quickstart), [Responses creation](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/methods/create), [model listing](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/models/methods/list), and [authentication options](https://developers.openai.com/codex/auth).\n\n"}, {"title": "Keys and settings", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#keys-and-settings", "text": "## Keys and settings\n\nThe password field holds the key only in memory. You can also set `OPENAI_API_KEY` for the OpenAI preset or `CUSTOM_AI_API_KEY` for compatible providers before launching the editor. Changing presets clears the previous provider’s typed key, then reads the corresponding environment variable. Save settings writes only API format, reasoning setting, endpoint, model, and limits to `my_ai_example_settings.json` beside the plugin. It does not save the key or conversations. Closing the window hides it, preserving the in-memory session and key until editor exit; reopening raises it. Change/remove the key directly in its field.\n\nRemote endpoints require HTTPS. Plain HTTP is accepted only for local loopback addresses. Credentials in URLs and redirects are rejected. Error bodies are not displayed, preventing accidental disclosure of provider response details. The editor makes one request at a time, only after Send. No live provider requests were needed to test this example.\n\n"}, {"title": "Limits and costs", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#limits-and-costs", "text": "## Limits and costs\n\n- Reply limit: default 1,024 requested tokens; adjustable 64–8,192. Provider/model support ultimately determines enforcement, and reasoning tokens may consume the reply allowance.\n- Input limit: default 65,536 UTF-8 bytes; adjustable 1,024–1,048,576. Includes system instructions, up to two previous complete turns, prompt, and attached document. Oversized input is rejected before sending, preserving the prompt.\n- Timeout: default 60 seconds; adjustable 5–180. This is a network/socket timeout, not a guaranteed total wall-clock deadline.\n- Request limit: default 20 HTTP attempts per editor session (including Refresh models); adjustable 1–1,000. Failed attempts count too. Reset history does not reset the request count. This counter is local to this plugin instance and resets on editor restart.\n- Responses are capped at 2 MiB. Empty or incompatible replies produce a notice, with no automatic retries.\n- HTTP 429 starts a local pause using a numeric Retry-After value, otherwise 30 seconds. It does not change provider quotas. A date-form Retry-After uses that fallback.\n- The provider's reported total tokens are shown after a successful response. The example does not estimate monetary costs or enforce a spending budget. Use provider-side credit/spending controls as well.\n\nA chat subscription is not an API key. Check your provider's API access and billing separately. Hiding the window or exiting the editor cannot undo an already accepted/billable request. The background worker uses no Tk calls; hiding the window leaves a request running, and cleanup ignores results after editor exit.\n\n"}, {"title": "Documents and replies", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#documents-and-replies", "text": "## Documents and replies\n\nUse **+ Files** in the composer to select up to four UTF-8 text/code files. Each file appears as a removable × chip; chips wrap in narrow windows. Duplicate paths are ignored. Contents are captured when attached, so later disk edits are not silently sent. To refresh a snapshot, remove and reattach it. Files persist across messages and reopening the chat until removed or the editor exits. Attachment contents are not saved in settings.\n\nThe file reader is bounded by the configured input-byte limit. Binary files, PDFs, images, invalid UTF-8, oversized files, and selections exceeding the four-file cap are rejected while keeping the existing attachments. At Send, all file headers, contents, optional active-tab snapshot, prompt, system instructions, and history must fit the shared input budget. Rejected requests keep the prompt and attachments unchanged. Attachments never send merely because they are selected.\n\nRemoving a chip stops adding that file to new messages. Content sent in an earlier turn can still be present in conversation history; use **Reset context** to clear that history. The visible transcript lists attachment names rather than their full contents, so Copy conversation does not copy hidden file bodies. Previously sent context may still influence replies.\n\nAttach current tab is off by default. When checked, it takes a snapshot of the active tab at Send, including unsaved edits. No unselected files are read or attached automatically. The transcript marks an attachment without copying its full contents into the visible conversation. Only successful turns are kept in memory, separated by endpoint/model/mode. Reset history clears model context but keeps the visible transcript and request counter.\n\nOpen reply in new tab copies the latest successful text into a new unsaved document. It does not execute the reply or install it as a plugin. Save and test generated source before loading it.\n\n"}, {"title": "Make your own version using the existing AI tool", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#make-your-own-version-using-the-existing-ai-tool", "text": "## Make your own version using the existing AI tool\n\n1. Open the example source and `PLUGIN_DEVELOPMENT.md`.\n2. In your existing AI Chat/Code, attach the source and documentation explicitly. Its smaller input limit may require attaching relevant sections or increasing its limits through a separate change.\n3. Use [BUILD_PROMPT.md](BUILD_PROMPT.md) as the request. Never attach actual API keys.\n4. Save the generated plugin under a new filename such as `my_custom_ai.py`. Change its menu label and custom event namespace so both versions can coexist.\n5. Use Plugin Manager to load it, inspect its services/errors, and disable versions you no longer need. Restart after editing an already-loaded plugin.\n\nCustomization points:\n- `SYSTEM_PROMPTS`: Chat and Code behavior.\n- `DEFAULTS` and `validate_config`: defaults and local limits.\n- `build_messages`: context and history policy.\n- `send_request` / `request_json`: Responses or Chat Completions payloads, reply parsing, and authentication.\n- `list_models`: account model-catalog discovery; it does not guarantee a model supports the selected API or has generation permissions.\n- `ExampleAI.show`: interface and controls.\n- `worker` / `poll`: background work, error handling, and GUI updates.\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugin communication", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#plugin-communication", "text": "## Plugin communication\n\nOther plugins can call `api.call_service(\"my_ai_example.get_status\")` to read `busy`, `requests_used`, `request_limit`, and `cooldown_seconds`.\n\nSubscribe with `api.on(\"my_ai_example.reply\", callback)`. A successful reply event contains `source`, `text`, and `model`. Event delivery is queued on the GUI thread. This is a deliberate text-sharing integration with loaded plugins, so consider attached content when choosing which plugins to load. Copying this example under a new filename changes the qualified service prefix automatically; update the custom event name yourself.\n\n"}, {"title": "Test without spending API credits", "page": "Your own AI plugin", "url": "custom-ai.html#test-without-spending-api-credits", "text": "## Test without spending API credits\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_my_ai_example\n```\n\nTests use mocked HTTP responses and never contact a real provider. Real provider compatibility, account permissions, model behavior, and billing are not verified by the offline tests.\n"}, {"title": "Pocket Editor plugin development", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html", "text": "# Pocket Editor plugin development\n\n"}, {"title": "Start with Plugin Builder", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#start-with-plugin-builder", "text": "## Start with Plugin Builder\n\n1. Open Extensions → Plugin Builder… (restart the editor or choose Extensions → Load New Plugins if the builder is newly installed).\n2. Enter a unique plugin ID such as `my_tools`, a command label, and an optional description.\n3. Choose Simple command, Text transform, Tool window, or Events and services.\n4. Review Code preview. Previewing validates and compiles the generated source without running it.\n5. Click Create & load. The builder creates `pocket_editor_plugins/\u003cplugin_id>.py`, loads it, and opens its source in a file tab. Existing files are never overwritten.\n6. Run your command from Extensions. Add a shortcut button through Extensions → Add to toolbar.\n7. Edit the source and save it. Restart the editor to apply changes to a plugin already loaded. Load New Plugins loads only files not already loaded; it does not reload edited plugins.\n\nThe builder needs only Python and Tkinter. Templates are starting points, not a package installer or an AI code generator.\n\n"}, {"title": "Minimal plugin", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#minimal-plugin", "text": "## Minimal plugin\n\n```python\nfrom tkinter import messagebox\n\n\ndef register(api):\n    def run():\n        messagebox.showinfo(\"My command\", f\"{len(api.get_text()):,} characters\")\n\n    api.add_command(\"My command\", run)\n```\n\nPlace this file directly in `pocket_editor_plugins` beside `pocket_editor.py`. Files beginning with `_` are helpers and are not loaded as plugins. The filename stem is the plugin ID. Use a distinct command label so users can recognize your plugin.\n\nThe loader executes each Python file, then calls `register(api)`. Commands are installed after registration succeeds. Keep imports and registration quick; do expensive work when a command runs. A plugin runs with the editor's permissions: install code you trust. `--safe-mode` starts without plugins.\n\n"}, {"title": "Public PluginAPI reference", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#public-pluginapi-reference", "text": "## Public PluginAPI reference\n\n| API | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `api.plugin_id` | Plugin ID, derived from the filename stem. |\n| `api.add_command(label, callback)` | Register a nonempty menu label and a callable taking no arguments. Call during `register`. |\n| `api.get_text()` | Return the entire active tab as a string, excluding Tk's automatic trailing newline. |\n| `api.replace_text(text)` | Replace the active tab's text as one undo group. Marks it modified; does not save to disk. Requires a string. |\n| `api.get_path()` | Return the active tab's `pathlib.Path`, or `None` for an untitled tab. |\n| `api.list_plugins()` | Return sorted loaded plugin IDs as a tuple. |\n| `api.on(event, callback)` | Subscribe to an event; returns a callable that unsubscribes. Callback receives keyword arguments including `source`. |\n| `api.emit(event, **payload)` | Queue an event for idle delivery to loaded plugins. The editor supplies `source=api.plugin_id`; do not put `source` in payload. |\n| `api.provide_service(name, callback)` | Register a synchronous service named `plugin_id.name`. Names must be nonempty and contain no dot. Duplicate names in a plugin are rejected. |\n| `api.list_services()` | Return sorted qualified service names as a tuple. |\n| `api.call_service(\"plugin_id.name\", *args, **kwargs)` | Call a loaded service and return its result. Raises `LookupError` when unavailable. |\n| `api.on_cleanup(callback)` | Register a no-argument cleanup callback, run in reverse order on editor exit or failed plugin registration. |\n\nThese APIs and Tk operations must be used on the GUI thread. `list_plugins()` and `list_services()` reflect plugins that have finished loading; dependencies may not yet be available during registration. Resolve optional services when commands run and catch `LookupError`.\n\n"}, {"title": "Events", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#events", "text": "## Events\n\n```python\ndef register(api):\n    def saved(source, path, **payload):\n        print(\"Saved\", path, \"from\", source)\n\n    api.on(\"document_saved\", saved)\n```\n\n| Event | Source / payload |\n| --- | --- |\n| `document_changed` | Core source `editor`, no required extra fields; queued and coalesced for edits and tab switches. Some plugins also emit this event. |\n| `document_switched` | Core source `editor`, no extra fields; delivered when selecting another tab. |\n| `document_saved` | Core source `editor`, `path` string. Delivery is queued; another tab may be active by delivery time. |\n| `plugin_loaded` | Loaded plugin's ID as source, `plugin_id` string. |\n| `document_formatted` | Formatter plugin source; payload varies by formatter. |\n| `project_changed` | Project plugin source; payload varies, including file actions or project directory. |\n\nUse callbacks accepting `**payload` for compatibility with extra fields. Namespaced custom events such as `my_tools.completed` avoid collisions. Do not emit the same event unconditionally from its handler: that creates an endless feedback loop. Subscription cleanup is automatic; use the unsubscribe callable when you want to stop earlier.\n\n"}, {"title": "Services", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#services", "text": "## Services\n\n```python\ndef register(api):\n    api.provide_service(\"word_count\", lambda: len(api.get_text().split()))\n\n    def show_count():\n        try:\n            count = api.call_service(\"other_plugin.word_count\")\n        except LookupError:\n            return  # Optional dependency is not loaded.\n        print(count)\n\n    api.add_command(\"Use another plugin\", show_count)\n```\n\nService calls are synchronous. Keep them short and handle failures at the caller. There is no manifest, automatic dependency installation, or dependency-order resolver; plugin files load in filename order.\n\n"}, {"title": "Two plugins communicating", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#two-plugins-communicating", "text": "## Two plugins communicating\n\nThe builder's Plugin communication tab lists loaded plugins and their available services. Refresh services after loading another plugin. The Events and services template publishes `\u003cplugin_id>.word_count`, sends `\u003cplugin_id>.counted` when its command runs, and forwards saves as `\u003cplugin_id>.observed_save`.\n\nFor a complete example, create an Events and services plugin with ID `counter`. Then create `counter_view.py` with this code:\n\n```python\nfrom tkinter import messagebox\n\n\ndef register(api):\n    def counted(source, count, **payload):\n        # Called asynchronously after counter emits its counted event.\n        print(f\"{source} counted {count} words\")\n\n    api.on(\"counter.counted\", counted)\n\n    def show_count():\n        # Service calls return a result immediately on the GUI thread.\n        try:\n            count = api.call_service(\"counter.word_count\")\n        except LookupError:\n            messagebox.showinfo(\"Counter\", \"Load the counter plugin first.\")\n            return\n        messagebox.showinfo(\"Counter\", f\"{count:,} words in the active tab\")\n\n    api.add_command(\"Show shared word count\", show_count)\n```\n\nChoose Extensions → Load New Plugins. Run counter's command to send an event to `counter_view`; run Show shared word count to request counter's service. Both use the active file tab. Events carry notifications; services return requested values. Plugins need no direct Python imports of one another. Calls belong in command callbacks rather than module-level code, because the other plugin may still be loading.\n\n"}, {"title": "GUI tools and cleanup", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#gui-tools-and-cleanup", "text": "## GUI tools and cleanup\n\nThe Tool window template uses `api._editor.root`, an internal integration interface used by bundled plugins. This is distinct from the public API above and may change. Prefer `ttk` widgets to inherit the editor theme. Keep one window instance, raise it when the command is repeated, and register a cleanup callback to destroy it.\n\nThe editor's `api._editor.buffer` is a stable interface forwarding to the selected tab. Do not cache a tab's text, path, selection, or cursor and later assume that tab is still active. Read them when a command runs. Each tab keeps its own text, tags, undo history, and cursor. Plain-text saving does not preserve rich-text styles; use a formatted copy for those.\n\nFor slow work, capture inputs on the GUI thread, use a worker for computation or I/O, and return results through a queue polled with `root.after` from the GUI thread. Workers must not call Tk or PluginAPI methods. Before applying a result, verify the captured document generation and revision against `api._editor.document_generation` and `api._editor.document_revision`; discard stale results after edits or tab switches. These counters are internal integration details. Stop workers, cancel scheduled callbacks, close windows, and remove custom bindings in `on_cleanup`. Do not block shutdown indefinitely.\n\n"}, {"title": "Test and troubleshoot", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#test-and-troubleshoot", "text": "## Test and troubleshoot\n\n- Test a generated transform with `transform(\"Hello\")` before wiring up more UI.\n- Use a scratch file. Run the command, switch tabs, and check Undo, unsaved markers, and Save.\n- For windows, repeat the command and close/reopen the window; verify editor shutdown.\n- For services, check `list_services()` and handle missing dependencies.\n- A syntax/import/registration error appears in Plugin loading errors. Fix the file and restart, or use Load New Plugins if that file never loaded successfully.\n- A command exception appears in Plugin error. Registration failures roll back registered API commands/services/subscriptions and invoke registered cleanup callbacks; external side effects require your own cleanup.\n- If startup fails because of custom code, launch `python3 -B pocket_editor.py --safe-mode`, fix the plugin, and restart normally.\n\nRun the builder tests from the project directory on a graphical desktop:\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_plugin_builder\n```\n\n"}, {"title": "Inspect with Plugin Manager", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#inspect-with-plugin-manager", "text": "## Inspect with Plugin Manager\n\nSettings → Plugin Manager lists installed plugins and shows registered commands, subscribed events, callable services, and recorded registration/command errors. Enable or disable startup loading there. Disable takes effect after restart for already-loaded plugins; Load new plugins can load newly enabled or repaired plugins that have not loaded successfully yet. Safe mode still provides this manager through Settings. Inspect a plugin's services before connecting it to another plugin, and use the builder's Plugin communication tab for a combined service list.\n\n"}, {"title": "Optional project tools", "page": "Plugin development", "url": "plugin-development.html#optional-project-tools", "text": "## Optional project tools\n\n`git_project.ensure_repository(folder)` synchronously checks a local folder, initializes Git if missing, registers the actual working-tree root, and returns that root as a Path. It works before the Git window opens and does not clone, stage, commit, or contact a remote. Existing repositories are reused, including when a subfolder is supplied. Invalid existing Git metadata produces an error instead of being replaced.\n\n`git_project.get_repository()` returns the registered root or None. It does not return an uninitialized typed folder. `git_project.get_project()` is a compatibility alias. `git_project.open_project(folder)` prepares/registers the folder and opens the independent Git UI. Services run on the GUI thread; local setup commands use bounded timeouts and setup is refused while another Git operation is running.\n\n```python\nroot = api.call_service(\"git_project.ensure_repository\", project_folder)\n# Other plugins can retrieve the same registered root later:\nroot = api.call_service(\"git_project.get_repository\")\n```\n\nSubscribe to `git_project.repository_changed` to observe registration changes. The payload contains `path` (string) and `initialized` (bool), plus the usual `source`. Resolve optional services when a user invokes a command and handle LookupError if Git Project is unavailable. Handle ValueError, OSError, or RuntimeError for invalid paths, missing Git, and Git failures.\n\nThe Git plugin works without Project Explorer or Team Project. Project Explorer Actions → Git Project selects, prepares, and registers its chosen folder. `team_project.open_project(folder)` separately opens hosted project collaboration and refuses to switch an active connection.\n"}, {"title": "Build a custom AI plugin with AI Code", "page": "AI builder prompt", "url": "build-your-ai.html", "text": "# Build a custom AI plugin with AI Code\n\nCopy this prompt after attaching the relevant example source and PluginAPI documentation. Never attach real credentials.\n\nUse the attached Pocket Editor plugin example and the actual PluginAPI documentation to build my own AI Chat/Code plugin.\n\nCreate one Python file named my_custom_ai.py, with register(api) and menu command “My Custom AI…”. Keep configurable OpenAI Responses and compatible Chat Completions formats, provider presets, model refresh, and exact model ID selection. Preserve the different token-limit and response-parsing fields for each format. Retain Chat/Code modes, explicit active-tab attachment, independent conversation histories, background requests, and opening replies in new unsaved tabs.\n\nDo not embed credentials. Keep API keys in memory or an environment variable; exclude them from saved settings, history, and error messages. Make only explicit user-triggered requests. Keep reply-token, input-byte, network-timeout, and per-session request limits visible and validated. Explain that request/token caps do not enforce a monetary budget, and do not add automatic retries or fallback providers.\n\nUse ttk styles and the editor's fonts. All Tk and PluginAPI calls belong on the GUI thread. Clean up timers/windows and ignore worker results after shutdown. Preserve current files and save prompts.\n\nChange the settings filename, menu label, and custom reply event namespace to my_custom_ai. Publish a get_status service and a my_custom_ai.reply event. Do not invent new editor APIs or modify the bundled AI backend.\n\nReturn complete source, setup steps, provider limitations, and offline tests using mocked network responses. Do not claim tests were run unless you actually ran them. Ask me which native provider API to support before changing the transport to another API format.\n"}, {"title": "PHP tools and completion exercises", "page": "PHP tools & examples", "url": "php.html", "text": "# PHP tools and completion exercises\n\n"}, {"title": "PHP formatting and completion", "page": "PHP tools & examples", "url": "php.html#php-formatting-and-completion", "text": "## PHP formatting and completion\n\nTwo independent plugins are available in Extensions:\n\n- **Format PHP** formats the current PHP buffer in a worker, with cursor preservation and one-step Undo. It does not write to disk. Invalid PHP, tool failure, a 30-second timeout, or a changed/switched document leaves the active text intact.\n- **PHP Format Settings** selects line width and target PHP version. Editor tab width supplies the default indentation; project Prettier and EditorConfig settings override defaults.\n- **PHP Completion** (Ctrl+Space) requests suggestions. Completion also appears automatically while typing PHP identifiers, `->`, or `::`. Use arrows to select, Enter/Tab or a click to insert, and Escape to dismiss. Other file types retain their normal editing keys.\n- **PHP Completion Settings** controls automatic suggestions, typing delay (default 120 ms), minimum characters, result limit, project PHP version and extra library/framework-stub folders. Settings are validated and saved atomically per editor/project.\n- **PHP Completion Help** shows usage, workspace, worker phase, response timing and the last error. **Reindex PHP Project** rebuilds the workspace after external file changes.\n\nCompletion uses Intelephense's free completion features through LSP, not a regex symbol guesser. It resolves namespaces, class imports, aliases, grouped `use`, imported functions, Composer PSR-4 classes and included files present in the workspace. Standard LSP additional edits support inserted `use` declarations. The workspace comes from a containing Explorer project, then a Composer/git ancestor, then the file folder. Saved PHP files notify the server; Explorer filesystem operations trigger reindexing on the next request. The first request may take several seconds to index. PHP buffers above 2 MB are excluded. Numeric snippets, default parameter placeholders and choices are supported; Tab/Shift+Tab navigates parameters. Mirrored placeholders, snippet variables and transforms are rejected safely instead of modifying the document incorrectly.\n\nThe plugins activate only for PHP-family extensions, including `.php` and `.phtml`. No PHP interpreter or Composer executable is needed for formatting/completion. Executing PHP still requires PHP; no PHP runtime was available during this audit. Dependencies missing from a PHP application's vendor folder must be installed separately by that application's developer.\n\nThe tool packages are pinned in `tools/php/package.json` and `package-lock.json`. They are already installed on this development computer. To restore them after copying the editor to another computer, install Node.js and npm, then run from the editor directory:\n\n```sh\nnpm ci --prefix tools/php --ignore-scripts\n```\n\nNode.js 22+ is recommended because some transitive dependencies require it. The tested PHP features passed here with the existing Node.js 20.19.2, which produced engine warnings for those dependencies during installation. No system packages were installed. PHP dependencies are optional, occupy about 172 MB locally, and are separate from the lightweight editor core. `node_modules` is excluded from source control. Intelephense is proprietary software with free completion features; this integration does not use premium features or supply a licence key. Keep its licence with any permitted local installation.\n\nSources: [Prettier PHP](https://github.com/prettier/plugin-php), [Prettier API](https://prettier.io/docs/api), [Intelephense documentation and licence information](https://intelephense.com/docs), [Language Server Protocol](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.17/specification/).\n\n### PHP test document\n\n```sh\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py examples/php/demo.php\n```\n\nSee `examples/php/README.md` for completion exercises. Run both suites on a graphical desktop:\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_editor test_php\n```\n\nThe latest combined suite passed 29 tests on Debian. Windows/macOS UI, process handling, and tool installation still require platform verification.\n\n### Refined completion behavior\n\nThe PHP worker cancels obsolete requests, keeps only the latest waiting task, warms analysis after opening PHP files, and maintains a short-lived bounded exact-result cache. The UI checks document revision and cursor identity rather than copying the full document repeatedly while polling. Suggestions are ranked using prefix/case and LSP ordering. The popup provides scrolling, symbol kind/type, lazily resolved documentation when supported, response timing, and keyboard/page navigation. Escape cancels delayed/in-flight results as well as hiding the popup, so an older request cannot reopen it.\n\nProject indexes live under `tools/php/cache/`, excluded from source control. Concurrent sessions use a temporary workspace if the persistent cache is already locked. POSIX stale PID locks are recovered; read-only cache locations also fall back to temporary storage. No user source files are written by completion. Existing document font and toolbar shortcut preferences are preserved.\n\nFor your PHP code: open its folder with **Extensions → Open Project**, open a PHP document, then use **PHP Completion Settings → Add folder…** for libraries outside the project or framework stubs. Select the project's PHP version there. Composer dependencies must already be present. `use`, aliases, inheritance, traits, interfaces, enums, typed return values, PHPDoc array element types, PHPDoc magic methods, built-in functions and external library paths are covered by real-server tests. Highly dynamic `eval`, runtime-generated members, magic without annotations and framework-specific templates are not guaranteed; provide PHPDoc/stubs where needed. This is not a claim of complete support for every PHP program or framework.\n\n`examples/php/advanced_completion.php` adds advanced exercises. `audit/php-completion-performance.json` records the measured demo timings: first worker request 1,178.42 ms (a persisted index may be reused), five warm requests median 14.72 ms (range 4.74–155.55 ms), and ten exact-cache repeats median 0.17 ms. These worker measurements exclude the configurable typing debounce and Tk rendering and are not a performance guarantee for large projects. Only the bundled PHP fixtures and temporary external-library projects were available for validation; no separate user application was supplied.\n\n\n\n"}, {"title": "PHP example project", "page": "PHP tools & examples", "url": "php.html#php-example-project", "text": "## PHP example project\n\nOpen `demo.php` with Pocket Editor. Extensions → Format PHP formats the buffer when needed, and Edit → Undo restores a formatting change. It may already be formatted if you previously saved it. Save only when you want to update the sample file.\n\nFor completion exercises, add a new PHP line after the existing code (not inside a comment), type a prefix, then press Ctrl+Space:\n\n| Prefix | Expected completion | Source |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `$customer->disp` | `displayName` | `use PocketDemo\\Models\\Customer`, defined in `src/Models/Customer.php` |\n| `$greeter->gre` | `greetCustomer` | `WelcomeService` alias for `src/Services/Greeter.php` |\n| `WelcomeService::defaultG` | `defaultGreeting` | Static method on the imported alias |\n| `format_customer_l` | `format_customer_label` | `use function` from `includes/helpers.php` |\n\nThe first request indexes this small Composer project. Select with arrows and insert with Enter/Tab, or click a suggestion. Escape closes the popup. Function/method completions can insert parentheses and parameter placeholders. Tab/Shift+Tab moves between parameters; Escape ends placeholder navigation.\n\n`grouped.php` tests grouped class imports. `includes/legacy.php` supplies a global helper for require/include completion tests. `composer.json` supplies the PSR-4 namespace map without requiring Composer installation. The valid sample program uses explicit `require_once` statements for execution; running it requires a separate PHP interpreter.\n\nTo see changed-file tracking, edit and save an imported class, then request completion in `demo.php`. Use Extensions → Reindex PHP Project after filesystem changes made outside the editor.\n\n"}, {"title": "Your PHP application", "page": "PHP tools & examples", "url": "php.html#your-php-application", "text": "## Your PHP application\n\nUse Extensions → Open Project to select your application's folder. Open its PHP document and choose Extensions → PHP Completion Settings. Set the target PHP version and add extra source/library or framework-stub folders that live outside the project. Normal project files, Composer namespaces and installed vendor dependencies are discovered by the language server. No PHP application code is executed by completion.\n\n"}, {"title": "Advanced exercises", "page": "PHP tools & examples", "url": "php.html#advanced-exercises", "text": "## Advanced exercises\n\nOpen `advanced_completion.php`. Its comments show inheritance, traits, interface implementations, enum cases, PHPDoc magic methods, typed return chaining and built-in function examples. The actual declarations are in `src/Advanced/UserRepository.php`. Suggestions can be requested on a new PHP line with Ctrl+Space. To test nullable members inside a typed function, use `function example(?Customer $customer) { $customer?->disp` and request completion at the end (incomplete code is allowed while editing, but must be fixed before formatting/executing).\n\nThe popup shows symbol type and available documentation, with scrolling and Page Up/Page Down. Default automatic delay is 120 ms; tune it or disable automatic suggestions in PHP Completion Settings. Completion runs only for PHP-family files, preserving normal keys in other documents. Dynamic framework methods require useful type declarations, PHPDoc or stubs; not every runtime-generated API can be inferred.\n"}, {"title": "Shortcuts, settings, and troubleshooting", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html", "text": "# Shortcuts, settings, and troubleshooting\n\n"}, {"title": "Keyboard shortcuts", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html#keyboard-shortcuts", "text": "## Keyboard shortcuts\n\nOn macOS, Cmd replaces Ctrl for the editor shortcuts below. Plugin-specific shortcuts retain their documented behavior.\n\n| Shortcut | Action |\n| --- | --- |\n| Ctrl+N / Ctrl+O | New document / open files |\n| Ctrl+S / Ctrl+Shift+S | Save / Save As |\n| Ctrl+Shift+A | Save All |\n| Ctrl+W / Ctrl+Q | Close file / quit |\n| Ctrl+P | Quick Open |\n| Ctrl+Shift+P | Command palette |\n| Ctrl+F / Ctrl+G | Find / Find Next |\n| Ctrl+Space | PHP completion in PHP documents |\n| Enter / Shift+Enter | Send / new line in AI composers |\n\nUndo, Redo, Cut, Copy, Paste, and Select All are also available in Edit. Split commands are under View; they have no dedicated default shortcut.\n\n"}, {"title": "Command-line launch", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html#command-line-launch", "text": "## Command-line launch\n\n```sh\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py path/to/file.py\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py --safe-mode\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py --no-session\n```\n\nSafe mode prevents plugin execution while retaining Plugin Manager through Settings. No-session skips both restoring and writing recovery snapshots.\n\n"}, {"title": "Settings and local files", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html#settings-and-local-files", "text": "## Settings and local files\n\n| File | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| pocket_editor_settings.json | Font, wrapping, line numbers, tab width, toolbar selection, disabled plugins |\n| editor_about.py | Published read-only author/contact details |\n| pocket_editor_session.json | Session tabs, cursor/scroll positions, unsaved text/formatting snapshots |\n| pocket_editor_session.lock | Session ownership lock; another editor instance avoids overwriting recovery data |\n| pocket_editor_session.invalid.json | Preserved malformed recovery snapshot |\n| pocket_editor_plugins/project_terminal_settings.json | Saved project commands and global _appearance preferences |\n| pocket_editor_plugins/my_ai_example_settings.json | Custom AI provider/model/API format/reasoning/limits; excludes API keys |\n| pocket_editor_plugins/code_theme_settings.json | Document theme and language selection |\n| pocket_editor_plugins/php_completion_settings.json | PHP completion project/library preferences |\n| pocket_editor_plugins/php_formatter_settings.json | PHP formatting preferences, when saved |\n| pocket_editor_plugins/python_formatter_settings.json | Optional Black formatting preferences |\n\nPlugin setting files are created when needed. Recovery snapshots contain local document content. Custom AI keys come from its memory-only field or environment variables, not from its settings file. The bundled AI backend manages its credentials separately; consult its `/keys` setup.\n\n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html#troubleshooting", "text": "## Troubleshooting\n\n- **A plugin does not appear:** use Load New Plugins, check Plugin Manager for errors/disabled status, and restart after editing a loaded plugin.\n- **A plugin breaks startup:** launch with --safe-mode, disable it through Settings → Plugin Manager, and restart normally.\n- **Text is unsaved:** a dot/amber title marks edits. Closing prompts Save, Discard, or Cancel. Save As cancellation and failed writes keep the file open.\n- **Formatting vanished after saving text:** use Rich Text → Save Formatted Copy for .pocket output. Plain-text Save cannot retain formatting.\n- **Terminal text is uncolored:** select a preset, enable ANSI or category coloring, or explicitly request force-color if your tool suppresses ANSI for piped output. Check encoding for legacy Windows tools.\n- **An AI reply ends early:** inspect the reply cap, provider errors, and timeout. Explicitly choose a larger cap if appropriate; this can use more credits.\n- **OpenAI rejects authentication:** use a Platform API key in My AI Example, not a Codex subscription login token. No credentials are read from Codex login files.\n- **Provider returns 429:** wait for the local pause, then check provider rate limits and available quota; retries cannot reset the account limit.\n- **Attachments exceed the input budget:** remove files/history or raise the limit explicitly. PDFs/images are not supported by the custom example.\n- **PHP completion is missing:** confirm the file type, local Node tools, project folder, target PHP version, and library folders; reindex after external changes.\n- **Python formatting is unavailable:** install Black in the interpreter running Pocket Editor. It is optional; do not change the editor’s Python environment accidentally.\n\n"}, {"title": "Verification and scope", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html#verification-and-scope", "text": "## Verification and scope\n\nUse the tests relevant to your changes. They require a graphical desktop for real Tk checks.\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_editor\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_plugin_builder test_plugin_manager\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_my_ai_example test_ai_assistant\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_terminal_colors test_split_editor\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_quick_open test_command_palette test_editor_session\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_syntax_diagnostics test_tabs_about test_php\npython3 -B tools/documentation/build_docs.py\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_documentation\n```\n\nThe terminal and process integration tests were run on Linux; Windows/macOS runtime behavior is not verified. Mocked AI tests make no real provider requests. No project/framework license is assigned by this documentation.\n\n"}, {"title": "About", "page": "Shortcuts & troubleshooting", "url": "reference.html#about", "text": "## About\n\nPocket Editor by **Somi Douth**. Contact [somidouth@gmail.com](mailto:somidouth@gmail.com). Built with Python and Tkinter. Help → About Pocket Editor displays the published author and contact information; it has no editing control.\n"}, {"title": "Working together on one hosted project", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html", "text": "# Working together on one hosted project\n\n"}, {"title": "Public IP detection is available first", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#public-ip-detection-is-available-first", "text": "## Public IP detection is available first\n\nOpen Extensions → Team Project. The **Your Public IP — Host & Client** panel is at the top, before role selection. Click **Detect My Public IP** to display your public IPv4, then use Copy IP if needed. It works for either role before starting a host or joining a server and stays visible when Setup is collapsed. A client can still join using only the host invitation; its own public IP is not a required connection setting.\n\nIf a lookup fails, read the error shown directly below the button and click it again to retry. The plugin automatically tries alternative services if one has a DNS, connection, TLS, or invalid-response failure. IP discovery does not establish inbound reachability or open a listening port.\n\n"}, {"title": "Easy internet setup (temporary sessions)", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#easy-internet-setup-temporary-sessions", "text": "## Easy internet setup (temporary sessions)\n\nThe host can use Cloudflare Quick Tunnel to obtain an HTTPS relay address without a Cloudflare account, domain name, router forwarding rule, or client-side relay installation. The project remains on the host. Cloudflare carries the traffic and the app still requires the individual client keys.\n\n1. Choose **Host a project**, select the shared folder, save/close project tabs, then **Start Host**. Keep Listen IP at `0.0.0.0`, or use `127.0.0.1` for relay-only local access.\n2. Open **Easy Internet Setup…** from Setup or Project Copies.\n3. Review the detected OS and shared folder, then click **Start Internet Sharing**. The helper installs missing cloudflared in your user tools folder, verifies its official release checksum, and starts the relay. There is no sudo, Windows service, router change, or system firewall change.\n4. Wait for **HTTPS relay verified**. The plugin waits for the tunnel connection, then authenticates back to this host through the public address. An unverified address is not used in invitations.\n5. Generate/export the named client invitation as usual. Its server address is filled with the verified HTTPS relay URL. The client only loads the file, selects a local project folder, and clicks Join Team.\n6. Keep the host and relay running. Stop Sharing, Leave / Stop Host, or closing the editor stops the managed tunnel. Existing temporary invitation URLs then stop working.\n\nQuick Tunnels are temporary development/session tunnels, have no uptime guarantee, and change hostname on every restart. Export a fresh invitation after changing the tunnel. For a stable long-lived address, configure a named Cloudflare Tunnel separately. The app's chat uses polling rather than SSE, and its per-file limit remains 50 MiB. No email-interactive `--allowed-mail` flag is used because the app's client is non-interactive; application client keys provide its access check.\n\n### Two terminal commands instead\n\nThe setup panel displays commands using the current Python interpreter and project location. It can also save a `.sh` script on Linux/macOS or a `.ps1` script on Windows. Run these on the host computer, from the editor directory:\n\n```sh\npython3 tools/team_relay.py setup\npython3 tools/team_relay.py start --port 8765\n```\n\nUse the actual port displayed by Start Host if it differs. Windows users can run the same commands with `py` or use the PowerShell commands generated in the panel. On Linux/macOS the saved script is executable and can be started with `./setup-team-relay.sh`. The saved script uses that host's interpreter/location; generate it on the computer where it will run. If a shell policy blocks a saved script, use the displayed commands directly rather than changing global policy.\n\nThe script automatically detects Linux/macOS/Windows and CPU architecture. It downloads from the official Cloudflare GitHub release, checks SHA-256, and installs into the user tools directory. `python3 tools/team_relay.py check` shows the platform and installation; `setup --force` replaces the user-local cached executable. The terminal Start command requires the app host already running and prints its HTTPS URL; copy that URL into Client-facing server URL if you launched the tunnel outside the GUI. Ctrl+C stops the terminal tunnel. No host or client keys are embedded in saved scripts or command-line arguments. Known team-token and named-tunnel-token environment settings are removed from the managed quick-tunnel process.\n\nLinux setup has been exercised on this Debian host; Windows/macOS asset selection and generated commands are covered by tests, rather than native execution here. Network restrictions or a relay-provider outage can still prevent setup; the app reports the failure rather than exporting an unverified URL. Outbound HTTPS is needed for downloads, and cloudflared must be able to connect to its provider.\n\nSources: [Cloudflare Quick Tunnels](https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/get-started/quick-tunnels/), [official downloads](https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/downloads/).\n\n"}, {"title": "Start the server", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#start-the-server", "text": "## Start the server\n\n1. Restart Pocket Editor after updating the plugin. Open Extensions → Team Project.\n2. Select **Host a project**. Set Your name and a Group/room name. Choose the shared project folder and save/close its files before starting.\n3. The default Listen IP is `0.0.0.0`, which accepts LAN/VPN connections on your machine's interfaces. Keep port `8765` or choose another unused port, then click **Start Host**. The app generates the administration key, shows the actual listening port, and suggests a local-network invitation address. `0.0.0.0` is a listen setting, not the address clients should enter. Choose `127.0.0.1` only for same-computer testing.\n4. Open Project Copies → Client Keys & Invitations. Enter a teammate's name and click Generate Client Key.\n5. Review the automatically suggested **Client-facing server URL**, or enter your own reachable address, such as `http://192.168.1.20:8765` for a LAN/VPN or an HTTPS address for a public server. This must be reachable from the client's computer; the host's `127.0.0.1` address only reaches that same computer.\n   Use **Detect My Public IP** at the top of Team Project to look up your outbound public IPv4. The result also appears in the invitation controls. It runs only on demand and sends no project data or client key. Detection does not overwrite an existing server URL. If you have HTTPS configured for that public IP, set the HTTPS port (443 by default) and choose **Use HTTPS IP**. A domain name with valid HTTPS can also be entered manually.\n6. Choose Export Client Invitation and save a `.team-invite` file. Send that file to the named client using your chosen file-transfer method. It contains their key, server URL, group/room name, client name, and project name. It does not contain the host's administration key or local filesystem paths. Generate and export a separate invitation for each client. Export the invitation while the newly generated key is available; only its hash is stored on the server.\n\n\nThe host computer keeps the complete shared project, room history, attachments, and backups of files replaced by client uploads. It must stay running while clients connect. For public internet use, place the host behind an HTTPS reverse proxy and give clients the HTTPS address, or connect everyone through a private VPN. The plugin does not configure routers, certificates, or firewall rules.\n\n"}, {"title": "Join from each client", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#join-from-each-client", "text": "## Join from each client\n\n1. Open Extensions → Team Project, choose **Join as client** (the default), and click **Load Invitation…**. Loading an invitation also selects Client mode.\n2. Select the `.team-invite` file sent by the host. The plugin validates it and fills in the server URL, group/room, client name, and key.\n3. Choose a local folder for your project copy. An empty folder is best for a first join. Existing different files are preserved and reported as conflicts. The host cannot specify this local folder in the invitation.\n4. Click **Join Team**. Loading the file alone does not connect. On joining, the shared project copies into your chosen folder, and Project Explorer opens it when installed. Connection settings collapse to give the chat more room; use Setup to show them again. Details stays available from the toolbar.\n5. Edit locally, save, and close the project's tabs. Use Project Copies → Send Changes to Server to publish edits or new files. Other clients use Pull Project to receive them. Updates use these explicit actions; typing is local.\n\nYou can still enter the address, room, and key manually if needed.\n\nAll clients in the room can read the shared project and publish changes. Each key identifies one client for messages; it is not a separate file-permission role. The host can revoke a selected client key in Client Keys & Invitations. Revocation blocks subsequent requests; it does not erase previously downloaded copies.\n\n"}, {"title": "Understanding Host and Client IP addresses and ports", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#understanding-host-and-client-ip-addresses-and-ports", "text": "## Understanding Host and Client IP addresses and ports\n\nOnly the host needs a listening port. The client makes outgoing requests to the address in its invitation; its operating system selects a source port automatically. Clients do not need to enter their own IP/port or configure an incoming port-forwarding rule.\n\n| Detail | Host | Client |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Setup role | Host a project | Join as client |\n| Address to share | Reachable host LAN/VPN URL or public HTTPS URL | None; load the host invitation |\n| Listening port | Usually 8765 for the built-in host | None |\n| Local network IP | Suggested from the OS route | Suggested from the OS route |\n| Public IP | Optional Detect My Public IP | Optional Detect My Public IP |\n| Request source port | Visible for each named client in the host's client list | Automatic and changes between requests |\n\nClick **Details** for the role, local IP suggestion, host address/port, and connection-check information. The client can see its own address and source port as observed by the host. A reverse proxy may appear as that peer address, so the plugin does not treat forwarded headers as a verified client IP.\n\nOn the host, Client Keys & Invitations → Refresh Clients shows each named client's last observed peer IP, automatic source port, and request time. This data is held in memory for the current host session. Key access marked Active means the key is unrevoked; it is not an online-presence claim.\n\nOn a LAN/VPN, the host address is filled in automatically when a suitable local IP can be suggested. Check that teammates can reach the selected network. For public internet access, keep using the configured HTTPS endpoint or a private VPN; address detection does not configure routers or bypass CGNAT. The HTTPS port in an invitation can differ from the built-in host's listening port when a reverse proxy is used.\n\n"}, {"title": "Conflicts and file limits", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#conflicts-and-file-limits", "text": "## Conflicts and file limits\n\nIf Alice and Bob both change a file, Alice can send her version first. Bob's later upload is rejected as a conflict. His local text stays intact. Bob can keep a copy outside the shared folder, compare it with the current host version, and reconcile the changes. To reset a conflicting file to the server version, first move Bob's file out of his project folder, Pull Project, then apply the changes to that new copy and send again.\n\nThe host's selected folder is authoritative. Host-side disk edits appear in subsequent pulls. Close host file tabs before allowing clients to replace those files, and save local edits before syncing. Deletions are performed on the host; pulling does not delete extra local files, and Send Changes does not publish deletions. Automatic merging and simultaneous live typing are not implemented.\n\nA shared project is limited to 5000 files and 512 MiB total, with 50 MiB per file. Symbolic links, `.git`, `.hg`, `.svn`, `.ssh`, `.aws`, virtual environments, `node_modules`, `__pycache__`, `.env*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `id_rsa`, and `id_ed25519` are excluded. Choose a dedicated project folder containing the files you intend to share. Attachment and prior-version stores each have a separate 1 GiB limit.\n\n"}, {"title": "Team chat and media files", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#team-chat-and-media-files", "text": "## Team chat and media files\n\nUse Group Chat to send text. Enter or Ctrl+Enter sends; Shift+Enter inserts a new line. Incoming and outgoing messages have separate cards, with sender names, timestamps, and date separators. Shared attachments can be saved from their conversation cards or the Shared Files tab. Attach File shares an audio recording, video, image, document, or other file up to 50 MiB. Teammates open Shared Files, select the attachment, and choose Save Selected Attachment. Files are not automatically opened or executed. Audio/video support means file exchange, not live calls or built-in recording/playback.\n\nMessages and attachments remain on the host across restarts. A newly joined client reads room history from that host. Project copies are stored in each client's chosen folder. Sync baselines and server chat/history data use `$XDG_DATA_HOME/pocket-editor/team-chat`, or `~/.local/share/pocket-editor/team-chat` by default. Keep this data directory outside the shared project. Back up both the shared project folder and host data directory. The host key is not saved automatically: reuse it manually after restarting if desired. Existing unrevoked client keys remain valid because the host retains their hashes.\n\n"}, {"title": "Optional dedicated server", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#optional-dedicated-server", "text": "## Optional dedicated server\n\nFrom the editor directory, set a strong `POCKET_TEAM_TOKEN` in the server environment and run:\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m pocket_editor_plugins._team_chat --bind 0.0.0.0 --port 8765 --storage /path/outside/project/team-data --project /path/to/shared-project --room project\n```\n\nThe host can join from Pocket Editor using the administration token and manage client keys there. Keep that terminal process running. Use a LAN/VPN or provide HTTPS at a reverse proxy for internet clients.\n\n"}, {"title": "Independent Git Project plugin", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#independent-git-project-plugin", "text": "## Independent Git Project plugin\n\nOpen Extensions → Git Project, or Project Explorer → Actions → Git Project. This is a separate plugin and can work without Team Project or Project Explorer. Browse selects any project folder. Clone downloads an existing repository; Choosing a folder automatically initializes Git if absent and registers its repository root. Set Git Folder prepares a typed folder too. Use Remote URL → Set Origin to configure a remote. Save edits, Refresh, select changed files, and Commit Selected. Push publishes to the upstream; a first push can set origin as upstream automatically. Pull receives teammates' commits and only fast-forwards. Git authentication and author identity use your existing Git configuration. Close project tabs before Pull.\n\nUse **Git Project → Settings…** to set this repository’s author name, email, origin URL, line endings, file-mode tracking, and commit signing. Settings are saved to the repository config and reload on reopening the panel. Blank fields and Inherit use inherited/global defaults. The panel displays the effective author and uses existing SSH keys or credential helpers for authentication.\n\nOther plugins may call `git_project.ensure_repository(folder)` to prepare/register Git without opening a window, then `git_project.get_repository()` to obtain the registered working-tree root. `git_project.get_project()` is a compatibility alias. `git_project.open_project(folder)` also opens the Git tools. Selecting a subfolder of an existing repository uses its root and does not create a nested repository. Team Project likewise exposes `team_project.open_project(folder)` for launching hosted teamwork with a selected folder.\n\n"}, {"title": "Public IP detection and actual connectivity", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#public-ip-detection-and-actual-connectivity", "text": "## Public IP detection and actual connectivity\n\nPublic IP lookup tries [ipify](https://www.ipify.org/), [AWS CheckIP](https://checkip.amazonaws.com/), then [Cloudflare icanhazip](https://icanhazip.com/) over HTTPS. Each request uses a four-second timeout and a bounded response. Only the first valid public IPv4 is used; TLS verification remains enabled. The same lookup and result are used by the top panel, Details, and the invitation window. The built-in host listens on its selected bind address and port. Detecting an outbound public address does not test inbound reachability, open firewall/router ports, install TLS, or bypass CGNAT. A VPN or proxy can change which outbound address is detected.\n\nFor internet clients, use an HTTPS reverse proxy with a valid certificate and route it to the host. The address and port placed in an invitation must reach that HTTPS endpoint. For private networking, use a LAN/VPN address shared by the team instead. Do not assume an IP lookup alone makes a server reachable.\n\nHost mode now includes direct **Install Relay**, **Start Internet Sharing**, and **Stop Sharing** buttons in the main Internet Sharing panel. They perform the setup/start commands without a terminal or extra dialog. The panel stays visible when Setup collapses after starting the host; Start Internet Sharing becomes available once the host is running. Installation is also automatic when starting sharing if the tool is missing.\n\n"}, {"title": "Interactive continuous test on one computer", "page": "Team Projects & Git", "url": "team-work.html#interactive-continuous-test-on-one-computer", "text": "## Interactive continuous test on one computer\n\nRun `python3 -u -B tools/team_live_test.py` from the editor directory to open separate Host and Client windows plus a test-control window. It creates an isolated temporary project, issues a demo client key, joins locally, and checks chat in both directions, project pull/publish, and binary attachment transfers. After the first local pass it starts the HTTPS relay and reconnects the client through it. Checks then repeat with a 30-second interval until Stop Test, window closure, or Ctrl+C/SIGINT.\n\nYou can type messages or attach files in either demo window while it runs. Keep manual project files outside `_automation`, which is reserved for automated heartbeat files. The controller offers Check Now, Pause Checks, Test Internet Relay, Use Local Connection, and Stop Test. It shows the temporary folder containing `test.log`, `status.json`, and both project copies. Logs and demo files remain after stopping; normal projects and editor settings are not used.\n\nFor a finite local smoke test without public sharing, run `python3 -u -B tools/team_live_test.py --local-only --cycles 1`. The default continuous mode uses a temporary authenticated public tunnel for the demo project only.\n"}, {"title": "Pocket Editor", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html", "text": "# Pocket Editor\n\nCreated by **Somi Douth** · [somidouth@gmail.com](mailto:somidouth@gmail.com)\n\nA portable Python 3.9+ / Tkinter editor. The core editor needs only Tkinter. Python formatting optionally uses Black. The PHP plugins use a separate local Node.js tool installation (Prettier PHP and Intelephense).\n\n"}, {"title": "Launch", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#launch", "text": "## Launch\n\n```sh\ncd pocket-editor\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py\n```\n\nOpen a file with `python3 -B pocket_editor.py path/to/file.py`. Start without plugins with `python3 -B pocket_editor.py --safe-mode`.\n\nOn Windows use `py pocket_editor.py`; on macOS use `python3 pocket_editor.py` with a Python installation that includes Tkinter. These platforms have not yet been verified. Debian requires Tkinter (`python3-tk`). Black must be available in the interpreter running the editor; it was available on the development computer during verification.\n\n"}, {"title": "Appearance and preferences", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#appearance-and-preferences", "text": "## Appearance and preferences\n\nThe interface uses a consistent charcoal palette, a compact New/Open/Save toolbar, a compact Extensions dropdown and optional toolbar shortcuts, wrapping file tabs with close buttons and unsaved markers, and a detailed status bar. Settings → Preferences changes document font, tabs, line numbers, and wrapping. Document font changes do not alter UI or gutter fonts. Code Theme controls the document color scheme independently of the UI palette.\n\nExtensions → Add to toolbar lets you check only the commands you want as shortcuts. The toolbar starts empty, saves your selections, and stays on one row. Shortcuts that do not fit the window are available in More; all plugin commands remain in Extensions.\n\n"}, {"title": "Documents and settings", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#documents-and-settings", "text": "## Documents and settings\n\nOpen selects one or more files. Each file has its own tab, edits, undo history, cursor, and formatting. Opening the same file again selects its existing tab. New creates another untitled tab. Click × or press Ctrl+W (Cmd+W on macOS) to close the current file; modified files offer Save, Discard, or Cancel. Canceling Save As or a failed save keeps the file open. Save writes the selected file; File → Save All saves edited and untitled files. Quitting checks every open document. Plain-text saving still requires a formatted copy to preserve rich-text styles.\n\nPlain UTF-8 saves retain the detected BOM and newline convention. Mixed newline conventions are normalized on opening. Saves and settings use a temporary sibling file and atomic replacement; existing symlinks are followed.\n\nUse Rich Text → Save Formatted Copy for `.pocket` documents. File → Save writes plain text. Unsaved formatting is protected by a separate discard confirmation. Rich formatting operations do not have formatting undo; text changes have Tk undo/redo. Word `.docx` support is not implemented.\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugins", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#plugins", "text": "## Plugins\n\nPython files in `pocket_editor_plugins/` define `register(api)`. Plugin IDs are filename stems. Plugins load in filename order and repeated Load New Plugins does not register them twice.\n\nThe API supports commands, text access/replacement, paths, events, services, and discovery. `api.list_services()` returns qualified service names. `api.on_cleanup(callback)` registers cleanup for failed registration or editor exit. Event callbacks and service calls execute on the GUI thread. Event payload key `source` is reserved. Plugins are trusted Python code; plugins that access private editor internals must register cleanup for their own side effects.\n\nThe terminal is an output-only shell runner. Its output queue and retained text are bounded; retained text is limited to 200,000 characters and individual long lines retain their last 8,192 characters. Stop and application exit terminate the process group on POSIX. Deliberately detached processes are outside this guarantee. Windows process-tree handling needs platform verification.\n\nRegex searches run in a disposable Python process with a 0.5-second execution timeout and a 100,000-match limit. A search may briefly pause the GUI while that bounded operation completes. Syntax highlighting pauses above 300,000 document characters.\n\n"}, {"title": "Verification", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#verification", "text": "## Verification\n\nFrom the project directory, on a graphical desktop:\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_editor\n```\n\nSee [release notes](RELEASE_NOTES.md) for the release scope and [the GUI preview](docs/assets/previews/gui-preview.png) for the updated appearance.\n\n"}, {"title": "PHP formatting and completion", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#php-formatting-and-completion", "text": "## PHP formatting and completion\n\nTwo independent plugins are available in Extensions:\n\n- **Format PHP** formats the current PHP buffer in a worker, with cursor preservation and one-step Undo. It does not write to disk. Invalid PHP, tool failure, a 30-second timeout, or a changed/switched document leaves the active text intact.\n- **PHP Format Settings** selects line width and target PHP version. Editor tab width supplies the default indentation; project Prettier and EditorConfig settings override defaults.\n- **PHP Completion** (Ctrl+Space) requests suggestions. Completion also appears automatically while typing PHP identifiers, `->`, or `::`. Use arrows to select, Enter/Tab or a click to insert, and Escape to dismiss. Other file types retain their normal editing keys.\n- **PHP Completion Settings** controls automatic suggestions, typing delay (default 120 ms), minimum characters, result limit, project PHP version and extra library/framework-stub folders. Settings are validated and saved atomically per editor/project.\n- **PHP Completion Help** shows usage, workspace, worker phase, response timing and the last error. **Reindex PHP Project** rebuilds the workspace after external file changes.\n\nCompletion uses Intelephense's free completion features through LSP, not a regex symbol guesser. It resolves namespaces, class imports, aliases, grouped `use`, imported functions, Composer PSR-4 classes and included files present in the workspace. Standard LSP additional edits support inserted `use` declarations. The workspace comes from a containing Explorer project, then a Composer/git ancestor, then the file folder. Saved PHP files notify the server; Explorer filesystem operations trigger reindexing on the next request. The first request may take several seconds to index. PHP buffers above 2 MB are excluded. Numeric snippets, default parameter placeholders and choices are supported; Tab/Shift+Tab navigates parameters. Mirrored placeholders, snippet variables and transforms are rejected safely instead of modifying the document incorrectly.\n\nThe plugins activate only for PHP-family extensions, including `.php` and `.phtml`. No PHP interpreter or Composer executable is needed for formatting/completion. Executing PHP still requires PHP; no PHP runtime was available during this audit. Dependencies missing from a PHP application's vendor folder must be installed separately by that application's developer.\n\nThe tool packages are pinned in `tools/php/package.json` and `package-lock.json`. They are already installed on this development computer. To restore them after copying the editor to another computer, install Node.js and npm, then run from the editor directory:\n\n```sh\nnpm ci --prefix tools/php --ignore-scripts\n```\n\nNode.js 22+ is recommended because some transitive dependencies require it. The tested PHP features passed here with the existing Node.js 20.19.2, which produced engine warnings for those dependencies during installation. No system packages were installed. PHP dependencies are optional, occupy about 172 MB locally, and are separate from the lightweight editor core. `node_modules` is excluded from source control. Intelephense is proprietary software with free completion features; this integration does not use premium features or supply a licence key. Keep its licence with any permitted local installation.\n\nSources: [Prettier PHP](https://github.com/prettier/plugin-php), [Prettier API](https://prettier.io/docs/api), [Intelephense documentation and licence information](https://intelephense.com/docs), [Language Server Protocol](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.17/specification/).\n\n### PHP test document\n\n```sh\npython3 -B pocket_editor.py examples/php/demo.php\n```\n\nSee `examples/php/README.md` for completion exercises. Run both suites on a graphical desktop:\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_editor test_php\n```\n\nThe latest combined suite passed 29 tests on Debian. Windows/macOS UI, process handling, and tool installation still require platform verification.\n\n### Refined completion behavior\n\nThe PHP worker cancels obsolete requests, keeps only the latest waiting task, warms analysis after opening PHP files, and maintains a short-lived bounded exact-result cache. The UI checks document revision and cursor identity rather than copying the full document repeatedly while polling. Suggestions are ranked using prefix/case and LSP ordering. The popup provides scrolling, symbol kind/type, lazily resolved documentation when supported, response timing, and keyboard/page navigation. Escape cancels delayed/in-flight results as well as hiding the popup, so an older request cannot reopen it.\n\nProject indexes live under `tools/php/cache/`, excluded from source control. Concurrent sessions use a temporary workspace if the persistent cache is already locked. POSIX stale PID locks are recovered; read-only cache locations also fall back to temporary storage. No user source files are written by completion. Existing document font and toolbar shortcut preferences are preserved.\n\nFor your PHP code: open its folder with **Extensions → Open Project**, open a PHP document, then use **PHP Completion Settings → Add folder…** for libraries outside the project or framework stubs. Select the project's PHP version there. Composer dependencies must already be present. `use`, aliases, inheritance, traits, interfaces, enums, typed return values, PHPDoc array element types, PHPDoc magic methods, built-in functions and external library paths are covered by real-server tests. Highly dynamic `eval`, runtime-generated members, magic without annotations and framework-specific templates are not guaranteed; provide PHPDoc/stubs where needed. This is not a claim of complete support for every PHP program or framework.\n\n`examples/php/advanced_completion.php` adds advanced exercises. `audit/php-completion-performance.json` records the measured demo timings: first worker request 1,178.42 ms (a persisted index may be reused), five warm requests median 14.72 ms (range 4.74–155.55 ms), and ten exact-cache repeats median 0.17 ms. These worker measurements exclude the configurable typing debounce and Tk rendering and are not a performance guarantee for large projects. Only the bundled PHP fixtures and temporary external-library projects were available for validation; no separate user application was supplied.\n\n"}, {"title": "AI Chat and Code", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#ai-chat-and-code", "text": "## AI Chat and Code\n\nThe AI window matches the editor’s charcoal theme, blue accents, compact controls, and UI font family, with Chat/Code navigation and a bottom\ncomposer, styled user/assistant cards, lightweight Markdown and code blocks,\nremovable attachment chips, and a welcome screen with draft starters. Enter sends;\nShift+Enter inserts a newline. The composer clears only after a request starts;\na failed start preserves the draft. AI fonts/styles are isolated from editor\nsettings. The sidebar collapses in narrow windows; More keeps Copy, Reset,\nRefresh models and Help accessible. Reset context clears model history while\nkeeping displayed messages available to copy. Copy preserves original Markdown\nand code text even when formatting markers are hidden in the chat display.\n\n[Conversation preview](docs/assets/previews/ai-chat-preview.png) ·\n[Welcome preview](docs/assets/previews/ai-chat-welcome.png) ·\n[Narrow-window preview](docs/assets/previews/ai-chat-narrow.png).\nThese previews use offline example messages; no provider request was sent.\n\n\nOpen **Extensions → AI Chat…** or **AI Code…**. The window has Chat/Code and\nAsk/Team selectors. Ask requires one model and a question. Team shows a lead\nselector and worker list; Ctrl-click workers from different developers, then\nenter the task. Workers run in displayed order. Refresh models reloads available\nmodels. Requests run in a separate process; Cancel stops that process.\n\nAttach current document sends a snapshot including unsaved edits. Attach files\nadds UTF-8 files. Attachments remain until cleared; no document is sent implicitly.\nReplies appear in the output pane and never overwrite a document. Copy conversation\ncopies the retained visible prompts, replies and team output, excluding hidden\nattachment text. Solo histories are separate per mode/model; the latest six turns\nare stored during the session, and Reset conversations clears them. Requests send\nonly the recent complete turns that fit the active fast profile. Omitted history\nis noted in the transcript; earlier visible conversation remains copyable. Team\nruns are independent.\n\nThe plugin uses these conservative **fast presets**, rather than claiming a\nuniversal optimum for every model:\n\n| Limit | Chat | Code |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Task + attachments, UTF-8 | 4 KiB | 12 KiB Ask / 6 KiB Team |\n| Requested reply cap | 512 tokens | 1,536 tokens |\n| Outgoing history | Up to 2 turns / 4 KiB | Up to 3 turns / 8 KiB |\n| Attachments | 2 | 4 |\n| Team workers, excluding lead | 2 | 2 |\n| Ask timeout | 60 seconds | 120 seconds |\n| Team timeout | 120 seconds | 180 seconds |\n\nThe active limits and input usage are shown in the AI window. Input byte counts\ninclude prompt, file headers and separators. Oversized tasks/files are rejected\nwith an explanation, preserving the prompt and previous attachments. Large files\nare read only up to a bounded size. A team request whose source plus Python syntax\ncheck metadata exceeds the backend source budget is rejected before model calls.\nExisting provider-size retry behavior may shorten team context with a visible\nbackend notice. Reply caps can leave large answers incomplete; Code asks for\nfocused patches rather than full-file rewrites. Timeouts stop the local request\nprocess and keep displayed partial output. Model discovery has a 45-second limit.\nThe shared command-line scripts are not changed; the GUI bridge supplies reply\ncaps at runtime to solo, planning, worker and final-answer calls.\n\nAsk makes fewer model calls than Team. These presets target smaller requests and\nshorter replies; actual latency depends on the model/provider. No live-provider\nspeed benchmark was performed. General rationale: [OpenAI latency optimization](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization).\n\nThe plugin uses `tools/ai/chat_ai.py` and `tools/ai/code_ai.py` and their shared\ncredentials. Configure keys and Free plan confirmations via `/keys` in either\nscript before use. Launch the editor again, or select **Load New Plugins**.\nNo API request is made until opening the assistant (model discovery) or Send.\nRun offline coverage with `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_ai_assistant` on a desktop.\n\n### AI capabilities and provider failures\n\nThe AI backend now receives explicit instructions that Pocket Editor supplies only\nprompt/history/attached text. It has no tools to browse a project, read files on\nits own, write files, inspect the machine, run commands/tests, or commit changes.\nIt may explain attachments and propose patches for the user to apply. Any model\nclaim of direct local access is incorrect; model-generated claims do not grant\ncapabilities. Earlier misleading replies are not proof of tool access.\n\nThe normal fast reply caps remain 512 Chat / 1,536 Code tokens. If a model returns\nan empty visible reply, the plugin retries that same call **once**, with up to\n2,048 Chat / 3,072 Code output/reasoning tokens. A notice appears before the retry.\nThis can help reasoning models that exhaust a small output budget, but does not\nguarantee success and may use an extra request/quota. No hidden reasoning is\nshown as the final answer. A second empty result produces a readable error.\n\nHTTP 429 is not automatically retried. The UI displays a provider rate-limit\nmessage, preserves the draft when sending is blocked, and pauses new requests\nusing that provider for its Retry-After interval when available (at most one day)\nor 30 seconds otherwise. The pause is local and cannot reset a provider's quota;\nactual free-tier/shared-provider limits can last longer. Select a different\nprovider or wait. Provider messages do not expose raw Python tracebacks. Failed\nteam planning/finalization is marked as failed even when the command-line backend\ncatches the exception; worker-only failures allow a partial result with an\nexplicit status. A successful size-limit retry does not leave a stale failure.\n\nReplies and team logs use structured text events, so answer lines that resemble\nprotocol markers cannot be mistaken for model lists or history records. Normal\nfast limits, Copy, the chat theme and Stop remain in place. Shared backend scripts\nwere not modified.\n\nSources: [OpenRouter reasoning/output budgets](https://github.com/OpenRouterTeam/docs/blob/main/guides/best-practices/reasoning-tokens.mdx),\n[OpenRouter error handling](https://github.com/OpenRouterTeam/docs/blob/main/api_reference/errors-and-debugging.mdx).\nVerification used offline transports, not live provider requests; provider quota\nrecovery and the reported models' current availability remain unverified.\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugin Builder", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#plugin-builder", "text": "## Plugin Builder\n\nUse **Extensions → Plugin Builder…** to preview, create, and load a command, text transform, tool window, or event/service plugin. Existing plugin files are protected from overwriting. See [Plugin development and API reference](PLUGIN_DEVELOPMENT.md) for the tutorial, templates, events, services, cleanup, testing, and reload workflow.\n\n"}, {"title": "Command palette", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#command-palette", "text": "## Command palette\n\nPress **Ctrl+Shift+P** (**Cmd+Shift+P** on macOS), or choose **Edit → Command Palette…**. Type words from a command name or menu category, use ↑/↓ to select, and press Enter to run. Escape closes without making changes. Plugin commands and toolbar toggles appear automatically from the live menus; reopen the palette after loading new plugins. Disabled commands are excluded, and the palette works in safe mode.\n\nThe bundled **editor_commands** plugin adds 14 everyday commands: Duplicate Line / Selection, Move Lines Up/Down, Delete Lines, Trim Trailing Whitespace, Go to Line, Copy File/Folder Path, Next/Previous Tab, Close Other Tabs, and Zoom In/Out/Reset. Line commands act on selected lines or the cursor's line and support Undo. Close Other Tabs checks unsaved changes and stops on cancellation or save failure. Zoom changes persist in editor settings. Load New Plugins or restart to enable newly installed commands; they are available from Extensions and the palette.\n\n"}, {"title": "Session restore and draft recovery", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#session-restore-and-draft-recovery", "text": "## Session restore and draft recovery\n\nNormal application launch restores saved file tabs, the active tab, cursor positions, and vertical scroll positions. While the editor is running, a local snapshot is written every five seconds to `pocket_editor_session.json` beside its settings file. After an interrupted session, a prompt offers to restore unsaved text and rich-text styles; original files remain unchanged until you save. Edits made since the last snapshot may be lost. Undo history is retained while switching tabs, but is not restored across application restarts.\n\nNormal closing still asks about unsaved changes. Discarded edits and untitled documents are omitted from the clean session; saved files reopen from disk on the next launch. Missing files are skipped with a notice. Malformed snapshots are backed up as `pocket_editor_session.invalid.json`. A file lock keeps a second editor instance from overwriting the first instance's recovery data; that instance runs without session persistence. The snapshot contains local document contents, including unsaved drafts.\n\nUse `python3 -B pocket_editor.py --no-session` to launch without restoring or writing a session. A file supplied on the command line opens after session restoration. Run `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_editor_session` for the persistence and recovery checks.\n\n"}, {"title": "Quick Open", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#quick-open", "text": "## Quick Open\n\nPress **Ctrl+P** (**Cmd+P** on macOS), choose **File → Quick Open…**, or search for Quick Open in the command palette. Type part of a filename or relative path; multiple search words and case-insensitive matching are supported. Use ↑/↓ and Enter, or double-click a result. Escape cancels. Files already open select their existing tab, preserving edits.\n\nThe search root comes from Project Explorer's selected project, otherwise the current file's folder, otherwise a folder picker. With no current file, the picker remembers its last selected folder for that editor session. Reopening Quick Open refreshes the index. File discovery runs in a background thread and skips `.git`, `.hg`, `.svn`, `node_modules`, `__pycache__`, `.venv`, `venv`, and `vendor`; directory symlinks are not traversed. Up to 50,000 files and 20,000 directories are scanned, and the first 200 matching results are shown. If a limit is reached, narrow the project folder. Unreadable folders are reported in the picker footer.\n\nRun `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_quick_open test_command_palette` for discovery, cancellation, existing-tab reuse, and palette checks.\n\n"}, {"title": "Plugin Manager", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#plugin-manager", "text": "## Plugin Manager\n\nChoose **Extensions → Plugin Manager…**, **Settings → Plugin Manager…**, or find it through Ctrl+Shift+P. Search by plugin ID or status, select a plugin, and inspect its path, registered commands, subscribed events, services, and latest loading or command error. Open source opens the file in an editor tab. Refresh rescans installed plugins; Load new plugins retries failed registrations and loads newly enabled plugins that have not already loaded.\n\nEnable/Disable persists startup preferences in `disabled_plugins` in the editor settings. Disabling a loaded plugin takes effect after restarting; it does not remove running callbacks or existing menu commands mid-session. Enabling an unloaded plugin allows Load new plugins to load it. Edited plugins that already loaded require a restart. Failed settings writes roll back the requested toggle. Runtime errors remain visible until restart; successful registration clears a previous loading error.\n\nIn `--safe-mode`, use **Settings → Plugin Manager…** to disable a broken plugin, then restart normally. Loading remains disabled while in safe mode. The manager reports the current process's registered events and services, not a dependency manifest or every event a plugin might emit. Helpers beginning with `_` are excluded.\n\nRun `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_plugin_manager` for startup preferences, safe mode, plugin communication inspection, error retry, and settings-failure checks.\n\n"}, {"title": "Inline syntax diagnostics", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#inline-syntax-diagnostics", "text": "## Inline syntax diagnostics\n\nThe **syntax_diagnostics** plugin checks saved Python (`.py`, `.pyw`, `.pyi`) and JSON (`.json`) filenames after a 450 ms typing pause. Checks use the current unsaved buffer, run in a background worker, and never execute user code. The first syntax error gets a red underline and a clickable line/column message below the editor. Fixing the error clears the marker. Results from a changed or switched document are discarded.\n\nSearch the palette for **Check Syntax**, **Go to Syntax Error**, or **Toggle Automatic Syntax Checks**. Manual checks remain available when automatic checks are off; that toggle lasts for the current session. Untitled and unsupported filenames are not checked automatically. Checks skip files above 200,000 characters. Python uses the running interpreter's compiler; JSON uses Python's built-in JSON decoder. These are syntax checks, not type checking, undefined-variable analysis, or project-wide diagnostics. Undo, saving, and file contents are unaffected by checking.\n\nOther plugins can request an asynchronous check through `api.call_service(\"syntax_diagnostics.check\")`, inspect `api.call_service(\"syntax_diagnostics.get_status\")`, and subscribe with `api.on(\"syntax_diagnostics.updated\", callback)`. The completion event contains `source`, `path` (string or None), and `result` with a status/message and line/column for syntax errors. The check service queues work and returns None. Disable the plugin through Plugin Manager if needed.\n\nChoose Extensions → Load New Plugins or restart to enable it. Verify with `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_syntax_diagnostics`.\n\nThe Extensions toolbar dropdown groups commands by plugin to keep the menu compact. Add to toolbar uses the same plugin groups and shared checkmarks as the menubar. Plugins with more than 18 commands continue under More… rather than producing an oversized menu. Search Commands opens the command palette for direct access to every command. The menubar's Plugins menu remains available.\n\n"}, {"title": "Split editor", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#split-editor", "text": "## Split editor\n\nChoose **View → Split Editor Right**, or find that command with Ctrl+Shift+P. The current file stays on the left and another open file appears on the right. With only one document, the editor creates an empty second tab. Drag the divider to resize the panes. Both panes are editable and scroll independently, with line numbers and filename/unsaved indicators.\n\nClick a pane to activate its document. Save, Undo, palette commands, plugins, and syntax diagnostics use that active document. Choosing a tab already visible focuses its pane; another tab replaces the active pane's file. **View → Focus Other Pane** switches panes. **View → Close Split** returns to one pane without closing any tabs or discarding edits. Closing a file still checks unsaved changes and collapses the split when only one document remains. Font, tab width, wrapping, and line-number preferences apply to both panes. Session restore retains documents and cursor positions; the split arrangement itself is not restored.\n\nVerify with `python3 -B -m unittest -v test_split_editor`.\n\n"}, {"title": "Custom AI plugin example", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#custom-ai-plugin-example", "text": "## Custom AI plugin example\n\n**Extensions → My AI Example…** is an independent, customizable Chat/Code plugin with a configurable compatible endpoint, model, in-memory API key, explicit active-tab attachment, reply/input/time/request limits, and reply events/status services. It sends nothing at startup and does not change the existing free-model backend. Load New Plugins or restart to enable it.\n\nSee [the setup and customization guide](examples/custom_ai/README.md), [the prompt for building your own version with AI Code](examples/custom_ai/BUILD_PROMPT.md), and [the example source](pocket_editor_plugins/my_ai_example.py). Verification uses mocked responses rather than paid API calls. Local request/token caps are not monetary spending guarantees.\n\nThe custom AI example also supports **OpenAI Responses**. Choose Provider → OpenAI, enter an OpenAI Platform API key (or set `OPENAI_API_KEY`), refresh the model list, and choose a compatible text/coding model. The Responses transport uses `max_output_tokens`, optional reasoning effort, and `store: false`; existing compatible Chat Completions providers remain available. Keys stay out of saved settings. Model refresh and generation are explicit actions and share the local request cap. See [the OpenAI setup steps](examples/custom_ai/README.md#test-with-an-openai-platform-api-key). Codex subscription logins and agent execution are not implemented by this plugin.\n\nThe custom AI window now uses a conversation-focused layout with a separate **Settings** window for providers/keys/limits, a welcome screen, styled message blocks and code fences, a bottom composer, and Copy conversation. Enter sends; Shift+Enter adds a new line. Welcome suggestions never send automatically.\n\nThe custom AI composer's **+ Files** button attaches up to four UTF-8 text/code snapshots with removable chips. Files and the optional active tab share the input budget with prompt/history. Binary/image/PDF uploads are unsupported. Remove and reattach to refresh a snapshot; Reset context clears previously sent file content from local conversation history. Selecting attachments makes no API request.\n\n"}, {"title": "Project Terminal colors and portable defaults", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#project-terminal-colors-and-portable-defaults", "text": "## Project Terminal colors and portable defaults\n\nChoose **Project Terminal → Settings…** inside the terminal, or search **Project Terminal Settings…** in the command palette. Editor Dark, Light, and High Contrast presets provide a starting point. Customize background, normal text, command headers, status messages, error exit messages, and all 16 ANSI base/bright colors. Font family/size, output encoding, ANSI rendering, and optional force-color behavior are user settings. Save & apply persists them under `_appearance` in `project_terminal_settings.json`, preserving saved per-project commands.\n\nPortable defaults use UTF-8 decoding, the operating system's monospace font, ANSI rendering enabled, and force-color disabled. The existing system-shell selection stays native to the OS; no Bash-only commands are introduced. If a chosen font is unavailable on another computer, the system monospace font is used. For Windows tools that emit a legacy encoding, choose locale, cp1252, cp850, or cp437 as appropriate. Python child processes receive the selected output encoding. Encoding and force-color changes apply to the next process; appearance changes recolor retained output immediately.\n\nThe renderer handles standard/bright ANSI colors, indexed 256-color and RGB colors, bold, underline, reverse, resets, and sequences split across read chunks. Other terminal controls, including title/clipboard/hyperlink escape commands, are discarded rather than executed; carriage-return progress updates become separate lines. This remains an output-only runner, not a full interactive terminal emulator. Standard error remains merged with standard output; category coloring uses recognizable text patterns rather than claiming to identify the originating stream. Nonzero exit markers use the configured error color.\n\nPrograms may suppress colors when output is piped. Enable **Request colored output from programs (force color)** to set FORCE_COLOR, CLICOLOR_FORCE, and TERM for the child; it also overrides NO_COLOR for that process. Some tools still require their own `--color=always` option. Disabling ANSI interpretation removes ANSI styling from retained text as well as future output. Output remains bounded to 200,000 characters and 8,192 characters per long line, with at most 512 distinct ANSI styles. No OS-specific ANSI rendering library is required.\n\nPlugin communication exposes `project_terminal.get_settings` and emits `terminal_settings_changed` after saving. ANSI interpretation follows the [xterm control-sequence reference](https://invisible-island.net/xterm/ctlseqs/ctlseqs.html). Parser tests are platform-independent; GUI/process tests were run on Linux. Windows/macOS runtime testing remains outstanding.\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_terminal_colors test_editor.EditorTests.test_terminal_output_fallback_stop_and_shutdown\n```\n\nThe main **AI Chat / AI Code** window now keeps the current reply intact instead of deleting its first 300 lines once the transcript exceeds 1,800 lines. Older transcript content is trimmed toward 200,000 characters without cutting the latest assistant/team reply; one large current reply can exceed that target. Code-fence formatting also survives consecutive assistant output chunks. **Reply tokens → Auto** keeps the existing conservative defaults; explicitly choose up to 8,192 for a larger requested reply cap. Provider limits/timeouts can still stop generation, and larger caps can increase usage. No extra provider request or larger default budget is introduced.\n\nTerminal presets now include **GNOME Tango** (inspired by the [GNOME Tango color palette](https://wiki.gnome.org/Design/OS/Colors)) and **Modern Graphite**, a softer dark scheme with restrained blue, green, amber, and magenta ANSI colors. Modern Graphite is the default for new settings; saved custom colors are preserved. The settings dialog previews command/error text and all 16 palette colors before Save & apply. Each preset applies its complete ANSI palette, not just its background. Restore defaults selects Modern Graphite.\n\n"}, {"title": "Terminal output categories", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#terminal-output-categories", "text": "## Terminal output categories\n\n**Terminal Settings → Categories** lets you choose separate colors for user commands/messages, system/info messages, warnings, errors, and successful completion. Automatic category coloring is enabled by default and can be disabled independently of ANSI rendering. Plain, unrecognized program output uses the normal foreground color. Settings apply to retained output as well as new lines.\n\nExamples of recognized prefixes are `USER:`, `INPUT:`, `SYSTEM:`, `INFO:`, `DEBUG:`, `WARNING:`, `ERROR:`, and `SUCCESS:` (also bracketed equivalents). Common compiler `file:line: error:` / `warning:` messages and build/test success lines are recognized. Matching is heuristic, not an assertion about process privileges or stdout/stderr origin. Partial lines are reevaluated as chunks arrive. The runner's own directory/status messages, command echoes, and exit markers have explicit categories.\n\nProgram-supplied ANSI colors take precedence over inferred category colors, matching the palette-driven model described in [GNOME Terminal's color documentation](https://help.gnome.org/gnome-terminal/app-colors.html). GNOME palettes alone do not provide this plugin's automatic log-category classification. You can use both features, disable automatic classification to see only application colors, or disable ANSI rendering to use only category colors.\n\n"}, {"title": "About and tab appearance", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#about-and-tab-appearance", "text": "## About and tab appearance\n\n**Help → About Pocket Editor…** (also in the command palette) shows **Somi Douth**, **somidouth@gmail.com**, a short editor description, and a Python/Tkinter technology credit. Publisher information is read-only; the About window provides Copy details and Close. Old local About override files do not replace the published author information.\n\nDocument tabs now use consistent active/inactive backgrounds and readable titles: white for selected clean files, muted light text for inactive files, and amber for unsaved files. Close buttons match the tab background. Selected, hovered, disabled, and inactive notebook tabs have explicit contrasting colors across plugin windows, including Terminal Settings and Plugin Builder. Split-pane headings use matching active/inactive title colors.\n\n"}, {"title": "Offline HTML documentation", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#offline-html-documentation", "text": "## Offline HTML documentation\n\nOpen [docs/index.html](docs/index.html) in a browser, or choose **Help → Documentation…** in the editor. The complete guide includes all bundled plugins, editor workflows, terminal categories/colors, both AI tools and provider setup, file attachments, PHP exercises, PluginAPI events/services, personal About information, shortcuts, local settings, and troubleshooting. Navigation, local search, code-copy buttons, light/dark themes, and print layouts need no server or external assets. Browser behavior under file URLs can restrict clipboard/storage; the guide falls back when those APIs are unavailable.\n\nRebuild after documentation or plugin command changes:\n\n```sh\npython3 -B tools/documentation/build_docs.py\npython3 -B -m unittest -v test_documentation\n```\n\nThe generator uses the Python standard library and reads source/Markdown without executing plugins. The HTML documentation is local; no website was published.\n\nProject Explorer: Open Project selects a folder. Save Project writes a `.pocket-project` definition; Save Project As writes another copy. Load Project reopens a saved definition. These commands are available in Extensions and the explorer Actions menu. Definitions store a relative folder path when possible so the folder and definition can move together. Document contents are saved separately with File → Save or Save All.\n\n"}, {"title": "Git Project plugin", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#git-project-plugin", "text": "## Git Project plugin\n\n**Extensions → Git Project** opens independent Git tools for any local project. Use Browse or Use Explorer to select a folder, or open it through Project Explorer → Actions → Git Project. Team Project is not required.\n\nClone creates a local repository copy. Choosing a folder initializes Git automatically when needed and registers the working-tree root for other plugins. Set Git Folder explicitly prepares a typed folder too. Enter a Remote URL and choose Set Origin to configure its shared repository. Refresh lists changed files; save document edits, select files, enter a message, and choose Commit Selected. Push publishes commits and configures the first upstream on origin when needed. Pull only fast-forwards and requires project tabs closed and local changes committed. Git authentication and author identity use your existing Git configuration.\n\n**Git Project → Settings…** edits repository-local author name/email, origin URL, line-ending handling (`core.autocrlf`), executable-bit tracking, and GPG commit signing. Reload reads the selected repository; Save Settings persists changes in its Git config. Blank fields and Inherit remove local overrides and fall back to inherited Git settings. Saves are atomic, preserve unrelated options, and respect Git’s configuration lock. Global Git configuration is unchanged.\n\nOther plugins can call `git_project.ensure_repository(folder)` to initialize Git if absent and register its root, without opening a window. `git_project.get_repository()` retrieves the registered root; `get_project()` is a compatibility alias. `git_project.open_project(folder)` prepares the folder and opens Git tools. Project Explorer remains optional; Browse works independently.\n\n"}, {"title": "Team Project, client invitations, and group chat", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#team-project-client-invitations-and-group-chat", "text": "## Team Project, client invitations, and group chat\n\n**Extensions → Team Project** opens hosted project collaboration, group chat, and shared files. Project Explorer → Actions → Team Project opens it for the selected folder. See [Team setup guide](TEAM_WORK.md).\n\nChoose **Host a project** or **Join as client** in Team Project. The host selects a shared project folder, keeps the default LAN listen setting (`0.0.0.0:8765`) or chooses another port, and clicks Start Host. The actual port and a suggested LAN invitation address appear automatically. Under Project Copies → Client Keys & Invitations, generate a key for a named client, enter the client-facing server URL, and choose Export Client Invitation. Send the resulting `.team-invite` file to that client using your chosen transfer method. The file carries the server address, group/room, client name, client key, and project name. It excludes the host administration key and host-local paths.\n\n**Easy Internet Setup…** detects the host OS/CPU and can install checksum-verified cloudflared in the user's tools folder, then start a temporary Cloudflare HTTPS relay. Start Host first, open Easy Internet Setup from Setup or Project Copies, then click Start Internet Sharing. After the app verifies the relay against the authenticated host, the public URL is used automatically in client invitations. Clients need only the invitation; no relay installation or IP/port entry is needed on their computers. Stop Sharing or stopping the host stops the owned tunnel. Temporary URLs change after restart and have no uptime guarantee; use a named tunnel for a stable production address. The setup panel also exports Linux/macOS shell or Windows PowerShell commands/scripts. See [the full setup guide](TEAM_WORK.md).\n\n**Detect My Public IP** is at the top of Team Project, before Host/Client setup, and remains visible when Setup is collapsed. Either role can use it immediately without connecting or opening another window. Copy IP copies the detected address. Lookup tries ipify, AWS CheckIP, then Cloudflare icanhazip over HTTPS, using a four-second timeout for each request and the first valid public IPv4 result. DNS failures, connection errors, TLS verification failures, and invalid responses trigger a fallback; if all fail, the visible message identifies the problem and the button can be retried. No project content or join key is sent, and lookup runs only when clicked. The same result is shared with Details and Client Keys & Invitations. Use HTTPS IP explicitly applies it to an invitation; existing server URLs are preserved until then.\n\nThe client needs only the host invitation and a local folder; its own outgoing port is automatic. The client clicks Load Invitation, selects that file, chooses their local project-copy folder, then clicks Join Team. Loading a file does not connect automatically. The initial shared project downloads on joining. Pull Project receives updates; Send Changes to Server publishes saved edits. Conflicting files are preserved locally and reported. Close project tabs before syncing.\n\n**Details** shows the host endpoint, suggested local IP, and the client source address observed during a connection check. Optional public-IP lookup is available to either role. The host can refresh its client list to see last observed IPs, automatic source ports, and request times. Proxies may appear as peer addresses; client source ports are not listening ports.\n\nThe group conversation uses incoming/outgoing message cards, sender names, timestamps, date separators, and attachment cards with Save actions. Enter sends; Shift+Enter inserts a new line. Connection settings collapse after joining. Audio, video, and other files can be exchanged up to 50 MiB each, with a Shared Files tab for the attachment list. Recorded media is exchanged as files; live calls are not provided.\n\nThe host retains history, attachments, and prior versions of files replaced by uploads. Each client key can be revoked. Invitations contain a client access credential and should be given only to that client. Chat hosting lasts until Stop Host or editor exit; a dedicated server is also supported. LAN/VPN addresses can use private HTTP; internet access needs HTTPS through a proxy. Git Project is a separate optional plugin.\n\nHost mode now includes direct **Install Relay**, **Start Internet Sharing**, and **Stop Sharing** buttons in the main Internet Sharing panel. They perform the setup/start commands without a terminal or extra dialog. The panel stays visible when Setup collapses after starting the host; Start Internet Sharing becomes available once the host is running. Installation is also automatic when starting sharing if the tool is missing.\n\n"}, {"title": "Source release", "page": "Full project handbook", "url": "handbook.html#source-release", "text": "## Source release\n\nAuthor: **Somi Douth** · [somidouth@gmail.com](mailto:somidouth@gmail.com).\n\nBuild a portable source archive with `python3 -B tools/build_release.py`. It includes the editor, plugins, packaged AI backend source, optional PHP dependency manifests, examples, tests, and offline Help. It excludes private settings, recovery drafts, invitation keys, caches, installed packages/executables, old backups, and local demo projects. See [Publishing](PUBLISHING.md) and [release notes](RELEASE_NOTES.md).\n"}];
